var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"manual/getting_started/#Low-Level-/-Expert-API","page":"Low-Level API","text":"This page documents the older low-level stepping surface built around SimulationState, build_simulation, and explicit integrator specs.\n\nUse this layer when you need direct control over GPU-resident arrays, manual stepping, or debugging of execution details. Normal scripts should prefer the workflow API shown in the Quickstart.","title":"Low-Level / Expert API"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/getting_started/#1)-Install-and-verify-CUDA","page":"Low-Level API","text":"using Pkg\nPkg.add(\"ParticleDynamics\")\n\nusing CUDA\nCUDA.functional() || error(\"CUDA is not functional on this machine.\")\n\nParticleDynamics is GPU-only: simulation buffers stay GPU-resident, and the current implementation uses CUDA-backed arrays internally.","title":"1) Install and verify CUDA"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/getting_started/#2)-First-low-level-simulation-(2D,-tiny-and-fast)","page":"Low-Level API","text":"using ParticleDynamics: build_simulation, step!, nve, velocityverlet, baoab, eulerheun, eulermaruyama,\n    write_xyz!, write_observables_csv!, gsd_open, gsd_close, write_gsd_frame!\nusing CUDA\n\nN = 64\ndt = 2.0f-4\nsigma = 1.0f0\nrcut = Float32(2^(1/6)) * sigma  # WCA cutoff\n\nst = build_simulation(\n    N=N,\n    box=(40.0f0, 40.0f0),\n    cutoff=rcut,\n    skin=0.4f0,\n    cap=Int32(32),\n    neigh_interval=10,\n    epsilon=10.0f0,\n    sigma=sigma,\n    gamma=50.0f0,\n    temperature=1.0f0,\n    nonbonded=:wca,\n    precision=:f32,\n)\n\n# Assign a nonoverlapping configuration before the first step. The low-level\n# constructor allocates coordinates and defers building the neighbor list.\ncopyto!(st.rx, Float32[2 * mod(i - 1, 8) - 7 for i in 1:N])\ncopyto!(st.ry, Float32[2 * ((i - 1) ÷ 8) - 7 for i in 1:N])\n\n# Preferred stepping path: keep an explicit integrator spec and reuse it.\nvv = velocityverlet(st; gamma=50.0f0, temperature=1.0f0, dt=dt)\nfor _ in 1:200\n    step!(st, vv, dt; compute_energy=false)\nend\n\n@show st.step\n\nExpected result: st.step == 200 and finite state arrays.","title":"2) First low-level simulation (2D, tiny and fast)"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/getting_started/#3)-Explicit-integrator-selection","page":"Low-Level API","text":"# Deterministic NVE velocity Verlet\nnve_spec = nve(st; dt=dt)\nstep!(st, nve_spec, dt; compute_energy=true)\n\n# Langevin BAOAB\nbao = baoab(st; gamma=50.0f0, temperature=1.0f0, dt=dt)\nstep!(st, bao, dt; compute_energy=true)\n\n# Brownian Euler-Maruyama\nem = eulermaruyama(st; gamma=50.0f0, temperature=1.0f0, dt=dt)\nstep!(st, em, dt; compute_energy=false)\n\n# Brownian midpoint (Euler-Heun)\neh = eulerheun(st; gamma=50.0f0, temperature=1.0f0, dt=dt)\nstep!(st, eh, dt; compute_energy=false)\n\nExplicit specs are the stepping API. Stochastic parameters such as gamma, temperature, and OU correlation time belong to the integrator constructor, not to SimulationState.","title":"3) Explicit integrator selection"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/getting_started/#Important-runtime-policy","page":"Low-Level API","text":"For stochastic paths that divide by friction, gamma must be strictly positive.   Tests enforce informative errors for invalid gamma <= 0 usage on BAOAB/Brownian/EM paths.   Use nve(st; dt=...) for deterministic inertial dynamics instead of encoding NVE through zero-friction stochastic parameters.","title":"Important runtime policy"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/getting_started/#4)-Simple-output-writing","page":"Low-Level API","text":"write_xyz!(\"traj.xyz\", st, st.step)\nwrite_observables_csv!(\"obs.csv\", st.step; Epot=st.Epot, Ekin=st.Ekin, dq=st.dq)\n\ngsdh = gsd_open(\"traj.gsd\")\nwrite_gsd_frame!(gsdh, st; diameter=1.0f0, types_names=[\"A\"], step=st.step)\ngsd_close(gsdh)","title":"4) Simple output writing"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/getting_started/#5)-Reproducibility-basics","page":"Low-Level API","text":"using Random, CUDA\nRandom.seed!(0xBADC0DE)\nCUDA.seed!(UInt64(0xBADC0DE))\n\nThis gives reproducible runs at the statistical/moment level.   Bitwise-identical trajectories across different GPUs/toolchains are not guaranteed.\n\nAdvanced orchestration helpers that are not part of the default import surface remain available under qualified paths such as ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.","title":"5) Reproducibility basics"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/getting_started/#6)-Where-to-copy-realistic-parameter-sets","page":"Low-Level API","text":"Use repository examples as templates:\n\nexamples/2D_example.jl\nexamples/TwoT_2D_LD_BAOAB.jl\nexamples/TwoT_2D_BD_EH.jl\nexamples/2D_active_OU_brownian.jl\nexamples/3D_BD.jl\nexamples/3D_LJ_NVE.jl\n\nThese scripts contain production-like parameter scales; for quick iteration/tests, downscale N and total steps first.","title":"6) Where to copy realistic parameter sets"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/forces/#Forces","page":"Forces","text":"ParticleDynamics exposes high-level parameter and topology objects for bonded and nonbonded interactions. Force kernels themselves remain internal and CUDA-specific.","title":"Forces"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/forces/#Public-parameter-and-bond-API","page":"Forces","text":"","title":"Public parameter and bond API"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/forces/#Current-interaction-families","page":"Forces","text":"nonbonded Lennard-Jones\nnonbonded WCA\nnonbonded soft-repulsive\nharmonic bonds\nFENE bonds\nlong-range Coulomb: smooth PME, PPPM (AD/ik) and Ewald summation with prolates, see Electrostatics\n\nThe stable user surface is the parameter/topology layer plus build_simulation(...; nonbonded=..., bonding=..., bonds=...). nonbonded=:none builds a state without a short-range vdW term (for purely charged or purely bonded systems).","title":"Current interaction families"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/forces/#Lennard-Jones-energy-at-the-cutoff","page":"Forces","text":"vdw_modifier sets the Lennard-Jones energy convention at the cutoff (the GROMACS vdw-modifier names):\n\n:none (default): plain truncation, u(r) = 4ε[(σ/r)¹² − (σ/r)⁶] for r < r_c;\n:potential_shift: u(r) − u(r_c) for every pair inside its cutoff (per pair with pair matrices), the GROMACS default.\n\nForces and virials are identical for both, so molecular dynamics samples the same ensemble. Energies differ by u(r_c) per pair inside the cutoff. This matters wherever the energy itself drives or is compared:\n\nMonte Carlo volume moves sample the energy they are given;\nenergy totals compared against another code.\n\nPlain truncation with a 1 nm cutoff makes SPC/E water about 0.5% denser under Monte Carlo volume moves than under MD. The missing shift acts like roughly 100 bar of extra pressure.\n\nst = build_simulation(...; nonbonded=:lj, vdw_modifier=:potential_shift)\nset_vdw_modifier!(st, :none)    # switch later; energies are re-evaluated\n\nThe modifier applies on both nonbonded backends.","title":"Lennard-Jones energy at the cutoff"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/forces/#Nonbonded-backends","page":"Forces","text":"3D states choose how short-range pairs are traversed:\n\n:ell (default): per-particle neighbor rows, one thread per particle. The vdW kernel and the real-space Coulomb kernel each walk the rows. This is the fastest choice for short rows, e.g. Lennard-Jones fluids at r_c = 2.5σ (tens of neighbors per particle).\n:cluster: the cluster-pair engine. Particles are grouped into spatial clusters of 8. One kernel evaluates Lennard-Jones and the real-space Coulomb term over a Newton-3 list of 8×8 tiles, with exclusions and scaled pairs as bit masks, and the Ewald or prolate correction as a polynomial in r². With a mesh solver, the mesh transform runs concurrently with the pair kernel and the list maintenance. This is the fast choice for long rows, e.g. molecular systems with a 1 nm cutoff (~500 neighbors per atom). On 1,029,000 SPC/E atoms with LJ + PME at Δ = 1e-3 (RTX 3090, Float32), a complete force evaluation takes 37.3 ms with :ell and 4.5 ms with :cluster.\n\nDefault choice: attaching electrostatics (attach_electrostatics!, or an electrostatics force in the workflow) switches the state to :cluster, and detaching it returns to :ell. Uncharged systems stay on :ell.\n\nCharged states with soft repulsion, and 2D charged states, are not supported: the cluster backend has no soft-repulsion kernel, and electrostatics are 3D. In small boxes the cluster skin is capped so that the list radius stays within half the box.\n\nst = build_simulation(...; nonbonded_backend=:cluster)   # or later:\nset_nonbonded_backend!(st, :cluster; prune_buffer=0.05)\nnonbonded_backend(st)                                      # :cluster\n\nIn the workflow, pass neighborlist=ClusterPairList(buffer=0.1) to the nonbonded force (or to the electrostatics force when there is no vdW term).\n\nThe list radius is the largest interaction cutoff plus the skin (buffer). Between rebuilds the list is pruned to the cutoff plus prune_buffer (default half the skin). It is re-pruned when a particle has moved more than half the prune buffer and rebuilt when one has moved more than half the skin. The Coulomb and vdW cutoffs are independent: a short ESP real-space cutoff inside a longer LJ cutoff costs no extra traversal.\n\nThe cluster backend supports:\n\nno vdW term, LJ and WCA (uniform, pair table or per-particle σ);\nbond exclusions of the vdW term and collision counting;\nwith or without an electrostatics solver.\n\nUse :ell for soft repulsion and 2D. Both backends give the same forces to round-off: Float64 agrees to 1e-11 and Float32 to ~2e-6 relative, the latter from the polynomial Coulomb correction and the summation order.","title":"Nonbonded backends"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/forces/#Mixed-sizes-and-exclusions","page":"Forces","text":"The low-level sigma_particle LJ/WCA path uses Lorentz mixing and honors exclude_bonded_pairs for dense ELL, stencil ELL, and all-pairs neighbors. After manual size changes, configure neighbor cutoffs to cover the largest pair interaction and rebuild the list; invalidate_forces! alone does not resize cutoff ranges. All-pairs traversal avoids neighbor truncation but has quadratic cost in particle count.","title":"Mixed sizes and exclusions"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/forces/#FENE-domain","page":"Forces","text":"FENE requires finite k ≥ 0, positive finite R0 with a representable positive finite square, and finite minimum-image bond lengths strictly below R0. Evaluation throws DomainError identifying an incident particle if a bond reaches or exceeds the limit. The potential and force are not clamped or continued beyond the physical domain. Near the limit they diverge; choose the timestep and initial configuration accordingly.\n\nA GPU precheck completes before bonded outputs are accumulated. It adds a kernel, a small status allocation, and host synchronization to every FENE force evaluation. A failed simulation step invalidates its force cache but does not roll back positions, velocities, or earlier nonbonded output. Restore a valid configuration before resuming.","title":"FENE domain"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/forces/#Example-scripts","page":"Forces","text":"examples/2D_soft_repulsive.jl: soft-repulsive neighbor-list path\nexamples/3D_stencil_two_sizes.jl: mixed-size cutoff workflow\nbonded polymer examples under examples/ remain GPU-first today and should only be treated as future CPU-compatible candidates after a real CPU backend lands","title":"Example scripts"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/forces/#Related-pages","page":"Forces","text":"Simulation State\nObservables","title":"Related pages"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/forces/#ParticleDynamics.Definitions.LJParams","page":"Forces","text":"LJParams{T}(ϵ, σ, rcut)\n\nLennard-Jones parameters used by the nonbonded force kernels.\n\nArguments\n\nϵ: Depth of the LJ well (energy scale).\nσ: Particle diameter (sets the zero of the LJ potential).\nrcut: Pairwise cutoff distance. For the WCA branch this is set to 2^(1/6)σ as in examples/2D_allpairs_quicktest.jl and the 3D scripts.\n\nExamples\n\nparams = LJParams(Float32(10), Float32(1), Float32(2^(1/6)))\nst = build_simulation(N=256, box=(80f0, 80f0), cutoff=params.rcut,\n                      skin=0.4f0, cap=Int32(64),\n                      neigh_interval=10, use_neighborlist=false,\n                      epsilon=params.ϵ, sigma=params.σ,\n                      gamma=50f0, temperature=1f0,\n                      nonbonded=:wca, precision=:f32)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Definitions.LJParams"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/forces/#ParticleDynamics.Definitions.SoftRepulsiveParams","page":"Forces","text":"SoftRepulsiveParams{T}(ϵ, σ)\n\nParameters for the truncated harmonic repulsion used in the two-temperature soft-repulsive scripts (e.g. examples/TwoT_2D_LD_VV.jl with σ = 1.0, ϵ = 1e9). The cutoff equals σ.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Definitions.SoftRepulsiveParams"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/forces/#ParticleDynamics.Definitions.HarmonicBondParams","page":"Forces","text":"HarmonicBondParams{T}(k, r0)\n\nParameters for harmonic bonds U(r) = ½ k (r - r₀)². The polymer examples (examples/2D_polymer_bonded*.jl) use k = 300 and r0 = 1.0 to keep bead spacings near a single diameter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Definitions.HarmonicBondParams"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/forces/#ParticleDynamics.Definitions.FENEParams","page":"Forces","text":"FENEParams{T}(k, R0)\n\nFinite-extensible nonlinear elastic parameters, with potential U(r) = -½kR₀² log(1 - (r/R₀)²). examples/2D_polymer_bonded_BP.jl demonstrates k = 300, R0 = 1.5. Force evaluation requires finite k ≥ 0, a finite positive representable R0², and finite bond lengths r < R0. Overstretched bonds throw DomainError; force and energy are never clamped.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Definitions.FENEParams"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/forces/#ParticleDynamics.Definitions.BondPotential","page":"Forces","text":"BondPotential\n\nAbstract supertype for bonded potentials so SimulationState can store either HarmonicBond or FENEBond and dispatch bond kernels accordingly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Definitions.BondPotential"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/forces/#ParticleDynamics.Definitions.HarmonicBond","page":"Forces","text":"HarmonicBond(params)\n\nConcrete wrapper storing HarmonicBondParams. Construct via harmonic_bond so the element type matches the simulation precision.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Definitions.HarmonicBond"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/forces/#ParticleDynamics.Definitions.FENEBond","page":"Forces","text":"FENEBond(params)\n\nConcrete wrapper storing FENEParams. Use fene_bond to create instances whose parameter type (Float32/Float64) matches the simulation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Definitions.FENEBond"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/forces/#ParticleDynamics.Definitions.harmonic_bond","page":"Forces","text":"harmonic_bond(; k=100.0, r0=1.0)\n\nConvenience constructor that promotes the supplied spring constant and equilibrium distance to the simulation precision. The polymer demos use harmonic_bond(k=300, r0=1.0) for bead–spring chains.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Definitions.harmonic_bond"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/forces/#ParticleDynamics.Definitions.fene_bond","page":"Forces","text":"fene_bond(; k=300.0, r0=1.5)\n\nConvenience constructor for FENEBond. The 2D/3D FENE polymer examples draw directly from this helper with the default parameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Definitions.fene_bond"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/forces/#ParticleDynamics.BondedForces.BondList","page":"Forces","text":"BondList\n\nGPU adjacency list describing bead connectivity in build-time particle-id order. Construct it with build_bondlist. Each bond is stored at both endpoints; index gives each particle's zero-based starting offset in flat, and counts its degree.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.BondedForces.BondList"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/forces/#ParticleDynamics.BondedForces.build_bondlist","page":"Forces","text":"build_bondlist(N, bonds) -> BondList\n\nConstruct a GPU-ready bond list from a collection of (i, j) tuples (1-based). examples/2D_polymer_bonded.jl builds its chains via:\n\nchain = collect(zip(1:(n-1), 2:n))\nbond_list = build_bondlist(n, chain)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.BondedForces.build_bondlist"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/forces/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.set_vdw_modifier!","page":"Forces","text":"set_vdw_modifier!(st, modifier) -> st\n\nLennard-Jones energy convention at the cutoff (GROMACS vdw-modifier): :none truncates the pair energy at r_c; :potential_shift uses u(r) − u(r_c) for every pair inside its cutoff (per pair cutoff with pair matrices). Forces and virials are identical for both; energies differ by u(r_c) per pair inside the cutoff. Energy-driven samplers (Monte Carlo volume moves) and energy comparisons depend on the choice. Only Lennard-Jones states take :potential_shift (cell-list and cluster backends). Energies are invalidated.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.set_vdw_modifier!"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/forces/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.set_nonbonded_backend!","page":"Forces","text":"set_nonbonded_backend!(st, backend; prune_buffer=nothing, skin=nothing, list_intervals=nothing) -> st\n\nSelect the short-range nonbonded traversal of a 3D state.\n\n:ell (default): per-particle neighbor rows; the vdW kernel and the real-space Coulomb kernel each traverse them.\n:cluster: the fused cluster-pair engine (ClusterPairs) evaluates Lennard-Jones and the real-space Coulomb term (exclusions and scaled pairs included) in one kernel over a Newton-3 cluster-pair list; the mesh part of an attached electrostatics solver is unchanged. Supported vdW terms: none, uniform LJ and pair-table LJ, with bond exclusions of the LJ term. The dense neighbor list is released while the backend is active.\n\nThe cluster list radius is max(vdW cutoff, real_cutoff) + skin (the skin of the dense list the state was built with). Between rebuilds the list is pruned to the interaction cutoff plus prune_buffer (default skin/2) and re-pruned when a particle has moved more than half of it; prune_buffer=0 gives an exact list for fixed coordinates. skin replaces the list skin.\n\nlist_intervals=(nstlist, nstprune) (opt-in) replaces the displacement triggers by fixed intervals, as GROMACS's dual pair list with a Verlet buffer: the list is rebuilt every nstlist and pruned every nstprune steps (0: only at rebuilds). Exactness then rests on the buffers being large enough for the motion over those intervals (GROMACS sizes them from verlet-buffer-tolerance); a pair can enter the cutoff unseen when they are not.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.set_nonbonded_backend!"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/forces/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.nonbonded_backend","page":"Forces","text":"nonbonded_backend(st) -> Symbol\n\nThe short-range traversal of st: :ell or :cluster.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.nonbonded_backend"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/forces/#ParticleDynamics.Workflow.ClusterPairList","page":"Forces","text":"ClusterPairList(; buffer=0.1, prune_buffer=nothing)\n\nCluster-pair traversal for 3D Lennard-Jones (uniform or pair table) and real-space Coulomb: one fused kernel over a Newton-3 list of 8×8 particle tiles (see set_nonbonded_backend!). The list radius is the largest interaction cutoff plus buffer; between rebuilds the list is pruned to the cutoff plus prune_buffer (default buffer/2). Bond exclusions of the vdW term are not supported (use CellList).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Workflow.ClusterPairList"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/observables/#Observables","page":"Observables","text":"Observables are accumulated on device and exposed through a small public API for step-level diagnostics, virial accumulation, and thermostat exchange tracking.","title":"Observables"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/observables/#Public-observables-API","page":"Observables","text":"","title":"Public observables API"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/observables/#What-is-available","page":"Observables","text":"total potential and kinetic energy\nscalar virial and tensor virial\nheat/work accumulators used by stochastic and thermostat paths\nintegrator-specific metadata merged into the returned NamedTuple","title":"What is available"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/observables/#Collision-counting","page":"Observables","text":"Collision counting is also part of the current top-level API:","title":"Collision counting"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/observables/#Example-scripts","page":"Observables","text":"examples/3D_quicktest.jl: writes energies while stepping\nexamples/ collision-rate workflows: production-style diagnostics, still GPU-first today","title":"Example scripts"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/observables/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.collect_step_observables","page":"Observables","text":"collect_step_observables(st, spec) -> NamedTuple\n\nCollect universal diagnostics, integrator-specific diagnostics, and bath heat / entropy totals accumulated since the last reset of the relevant buffers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.collect_step_observables"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/observables/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.reset_bath_exchange_accumulators!","page":"Observables","text":"reset_bath_exchange_accumulators!(st, spec)\n\nReset the accumulators used for bath heat and entropy diagnostics. This leaves positions, velocities, and thermostat internal state untouched.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.reset_bath_exchange_accumulators!"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/observables/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.accumulate_virial!","page":"Observables","text":"accumulate_virial!(st)\n\nAdd the instantaneous virial trace and total virial tensor into their per-interval accumulators, including the global (reciprocal electrostatic) tensor. st.virial remains the scalar per-particle trace of the configurational virial tensor for backward compatibility.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.accumulate_virial!"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/observables/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.virial_components","page":"Observables","text":"virial_components(st)\n\nReturn the component ordering used by the current simulation state's virial tensors.\n\n2D: (:xx, :yy, :xy)\n3D: (:xx, :yy, :zz, :xy, :xz, :yz)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.virial_components"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/observables/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.virial_tensor","page":"Observables","text":"virial_tensor(st; part=:total, accumulated=false)\n\nReturn the particle-summed configurational virial tensor components as a NamedTuple. The stored sign convention is the raw configurational virial W = Σ r_ij ⊗ F_ij, so pressure/stress formulas may need an additional minus sign depending on convention.\n\nComponent ordering follows:\n\n2D: (:xx, :yy, :xy)\n3D: (:xx, :yy, :zz, :xy, :xz, :yz)\n\npart selects :total (per-particle terms, reciprocal-space electrostatic virial and rigid-water constraint virial), :nonbonded (vdW plus real-space Coulomb and exception corrections), :bonded, or :reciprocal (the global electrostatic mesh tensor only).\n\nThe primary buffers remain GPU-resident in SimulationState; this helper reduces the selected tensor to a small host-side summary for diagnostics. Virial buffers are refreshed by force evaluations with compute_energy=true. Rigid-water contributions are recomputed from current coordinates, velocities and conservative forces when sampled; they are not stored in st.virial.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.virial_tensor"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/observables/#ParticleDynamics.Collisions.enable_collision_counting!","page":"Observables","text":"enable_collision_counting!(st; ntypes=nothing, bins=:all_pairs)\n\nAttach GPU buffers that count contact-entry events (pairs whose separation crosses below the collision cutoff). examples/TwoT_2D_LD_VV.jl enables this with ntypes=2 to log cold/cold, cold/hot, and hot/hot encounters.\n\nArguments\n\nntypes: Number of particle types; defaults to maximum(Array(st.typeid)).\nbins: Currently only :all_pairs, which bins every unordered type pair.\n\nExamples\n\nenable_collision_counting!(st; ntypes=2, bins=:all_pairs)\nvv = velocityverlet(st; gamma=gamma, temperature=temperature, dt=dt)\nfor _ in 1:1_000_000\n    step!(st, vv, dt; compute_energy=false)\nend\ncounts = collisions_read_counts!(st) ./ (dt * 1_000_000)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Collisions.enable_collision_counting!"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/observables/#ParticleDynamics.Collisions.disable_collision_counting!","page":"Observables","text":"disable_collision_counting!(st)\n\nTear down all collision-counting buffers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Collisions.disable_collision_counting!"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/observables/#ParticleDynamics.Collisions.collisions_reset_counts!","page":"Observables","text":"collisions_reset_counts!(st)\n\nClear the histogram without reinitializing the contact state. Handy after a warmup period (mirrors the procedure in examples/TwoT_2D_LD_VV.jl).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Collisions.collisions_reset_counts!"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/observables/#ParticleDynamics.Collisions.collisions_read_counts!","page":"Observables","text":"collisions_read_counts!(st) -> Vector{Int64}\n\nCopy device counters to host. The vector ordering matches the bin assignment printed by enable_collision_counting! (unordered type pairs).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Collisions.collisions_read_counts!"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/observables/#ParticleDynamics.Collisions.set_collision_pair_cutoffs!","page":"Observables","text":"set_collision_pair_cutoffs!(st, rcut_pair)\n\nOverride the contact cutoff used for counting with an ntypes×ntypes matrix. Each entry stores the distance (not squared). This mirrors the RCUT_PAIR setup in examples/3D_stencil_two_sizes.jl.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Collisions.set_collision_pair_cutoffs!"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/electrostatics/#Electrostatics","page":"Electrostatics","text":"ParticleDynamics computes long-range Coulomb interactions natively on the GPU with three particle-mesh solvers that share one mesh engine:\n\nDescriptor Method Split Assignment Differentiation Transforms per force call\nPME smooth particle-mesh Ewald Gaussian cardinal B-spline analytic 1 forward, 1 inverse\nPPPM(differentiation=:ad) P3M, optimal influence function Gaussian B-spline analytic 1 forward, 1 inverse\nPPPM(differentiation=:ik) P3M, optimal influence function Gaussian B-spline reciprocal-space field 1 forward, 3 inverse\nESP Ewald summation with prolates prolate (PSWF) prolate window analytic 1 forward, 1 inverse\n\nEvery step of a force evaluation runs on the device: real-space pairs, exception corrections, charge spreading, cuFFT R2C/C2R transforms with preallocated plans, the spectral solve (with Float64 energy and virial reductions), and the force gather. Host code validates the input, generates coefficients and plans transforms at setup only. A warmed force call performs no device allocations.\n\nSupported scope: 3D orthorhombic periodic boxes, conducting (tin-foil) boundary conditions, uniform relative permittivity, fixed charges, a single GPU, Float32 or Float64. 2D, slab, triclinic and open-boundary electrostatics are rejected at construction.","title":"Electrostatics"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/electrostatics/#Quick-example","page":"Electrostatics","text":"using ParticleDynamics\n\nn, a = 16, 2.82                                  # Å\nL = n * a\npos = [(a * (i - 0.5) - L / 2, a * (j - 0.5) - L / 2, a * (k - 0.5) - L / 2)\n       for i in 1:n for j in 1:n for k in 1:n]\ntypeids = Int32[isodd(i + j + k) ? 1 : 2 for i in 1:n for j in 1:n for k in 1:n]\nsystem = ParticleSystem(pos; box=(L, L, L), types=[:Na, :Cl], typeids=typeids,\n                        masses=Dict(:Na => 22.99, :Cl => 35.45),\n                        charges=Dict(:Na => 1.0, :Cl => -1.0))\nall_ions = Group(:all, AllSelection())\n\nsim = Simulation(system;\n    groups=Groups(all_ions),\n    integrator=Integrator(\n        dt=0.1,                                   # in √(amu Å²/eV) ≈ 10.18 fs units\n        forces=[\n            LennardJones(epsilon=0.01, sigma=2.8, cutoff=9.0, pairs=:neighborlist,\n                         neighborlist=CellList(capacity=512)),\n            PME(coulomb_prefactor=14.399645,      # eV·Å/e²\n                real_cutoff=9.0, real_space_tolerance=1e-5,\n                mesh_spacing=1.0, order=5),\n        ],\n        methods=[ConstantVolume(all_ions)],\n    ),\n    observables=[ThermodynamicObservable(all_ions)],\n    precision=Float32,\n)\nrun!(sim, Stage(:nve, steps=1000; progress=false))\n\nReplace the PME(...) entry by PPPM(...; differentiation=:ik) or ESP(coulomb_prefactor=14.399645, real_cutoff=9.0, tolerance=1e-5) to switch methods. The short-range vdW force keeps its own cutoff; the shared neighbor list covers the larger of the two cutoffs.","title":"Quick example"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/electrostatics/#Units-and-conventions","page":"Electrostatics","text":"The engine has no built-in unit system. The pair energy is\n\nU_ij = Cfracq_i q_jr_ijqquad\nC = fractextttcoulomb_prefactorvarepsilon_r\n\nand C enters every term (real space, reciprocal space, self energy, exceptions, background, virial) exactly once.\n\nQuantity Unit\npositions, box, cutoffs, mesh_spacing L\nalpha (Gaussian split) 1/L\ncharges caller's Q\ncoulomb_prefactor E·L/Q²\nenergy, virial E\nforces E/L\n\nCommon choices: ≈138.935458 for nm, kJ/mol and elementary charges; ≈14.399645 for Å, eV and elementary charges; 1 for reduced units. Thermostat inputs stay kT in energy units. ESP shape parameters (split_shape, window_shape) are dimensionless and distinct from alpha.","title":"Units and conventions"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/electrostatics/#Ewald-split","page":"Electrostatics","text":"For a split kernel χ (dimensionless, ∫χ = 1) with a(t) = ∫₀ᵗ χ, t = r/r_c:\n\nfrac1r = frac1-a(t)r + fraca(t)rqquad\nhat g(k) = frac4pik^2int chi(x)cos(k r_c x)dx \n\nThe Gaussian choice gives erfc(αr)/r, ĝ = 4π e^{-k²/4α²}/k² and the self energy −Cα/√π Σqᵢ². The ESP choice uses the order-zero prolate spheroidal wave function ψ₀ᶜ on [0, 1], so the real-space part vanishes exactly at r_c. Its self energy is −(C/2) χ(0)/r_c Σqᵢ².","title":"Ewald split"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/electrostatics/#Mesh","page":"Electrostatics","text":"Charges are assigned per node with the window W (B-spline of order P, or the prolate window ψ₀ᶜ¹(2x/P)), transformed with an unnormalised forward FFT, multiplied by the influence function G (which contains C/V) and transformed back with an unnormalised inverse. The mesh energy is ½ Σ G|b̂|²; analytic-differentiation forces are the exact gradient of this discrete energy. Influence functions:\n\nSPME: G = (C/V) ĝ(k) Π_d D_d, with SPME's exact discrete B-spline factor D;\nP3M-AD/ik: Hockney–Eastwood optimal alias sums for the selected derivative;\nESP: by default the optimal AD influence function with alias sums of the prolate window transform (influence=:optimal). With influence=:deconvolution it is the plain ĝ/φ̂² of the paper's eq. (9).","title":"Mesh"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/electrostatics/#Energies,-virials-and-observables","page":"Electrostatics","text":"Per-particle energies. Real-space and exception pair energies are split ½/½. The mesh contributes ½ qᵢ uᵢ (uᵢ = interpolated mesh potential), which sums exactly to the mesh energy. The self energy is local, and a neutralising background is distributed in proportion to qᵢ Q. System totals (sum(st.Epot), thermodynamic observables, NHC/CSVR extended Hamiltonians) therefore include electrostatics automatically. Subgroup energies follow this documented convention.\nVirial. Real-space and exception virials are per-particle (in virial_nonbonded). The reciprocal-space tensor is the strain derivative of the discrete mesh energy at fixed fractional coordinates. It has no per-particle decomposition and is stored globally in st.virial_global. virial_tensor(st) and full-system observables include it; virial_tensor(st; part=:reciprocal) returns it alone. Group observables that request a virial for a strict subgroup raise an error while electrostatics is attached. Per-particle GSD virial output contains the local terms only.\nDiagnostics. electrostatic_energy returns the real, self, reciprocal and background components plus the reciprocal virial at the current coordinates.\n\nEnergies and virials are refreshed on force evaluations with compute_energy=true, like every other term in the engine.","title":"Energies, virials and observables"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/electrostatics/#Exceptions","page":"Electrostatics","text":"Scaled or excluded pairs (e.g. 1–2/1–3 exclusions, 1–4 scaling) are handled by subtracting (1 − s) C qᵢqⱼ/r at the pair's minimum-image distance, independently of the cutoff. Periodic images keep the full interaction. The real-space sum itself never skips pairs, and Lennard-Jones exclusions never delete Coulomb pairs. Workflow descriptors collect exceptions from exclusions=:topology (Topology.exclusions, scale 0), exclude_bonded=true, and scaled_pairs=[(i, j, s), …].","title":"Exceptions"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/electrostatics/#Charged-systems","page":"Electrostatics","text":"net_charge=:error (default) rejects a nonzero total charge. With net_charge=:background a uniform neutralising background adds −C π Q²/(2α²V) (Gaussian) or −C π r_c² m₂ Q²/V (prolate). It contributes the same amount to each diagonal virial component and adds no force.","title":"Charged systems"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/electrostatics/#Lifecycle,-reordering-and-restarts","page":"Electrostatics","text":"The solver holds no particle-array pointers; positions, charges and force slots are passed on every call. Force-slot swaps, Brownian midpoint evaluations and spatial reordering are therefore safe. Charges are permuted with their particles. Reordering is suspended while exception rows are attached.\nset_charges! replaces charges and re-checks the net-charge policy. detach_electrostatics! removes the solver.\nGSD frames written while a solver is attached store particles/charge and log/electrostatics/settings (method, prefactor, εᵣ, cutoff, α or shapes, order, mesh, alias order, net-charge policy, precision), plus log/electrostatics/exceptions. ParticleSystem.from_gsd(path) restores the charges, and electrostatics_from_gsd returns the exact descriptor. FFT plans and workspaces are rebuilt on load.","title":"Lifecycle, reordering and restarts"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/electrostatics/#Choosing-parameters","page":"Electrostatics","text":"Parameters are explicit; nothing is tuned behind the user's back.\n\nPME/PPPM. Choose real_cutoff, then alpha, or real_space_tolerance (erfc(α r_c) = tol, a real-space potential-tail criterion rather than a force error). Then refine mesh/mesh_spacing and order until the measured force error meets the target.\nESP. tolerance = ε sets the split shape (ψ₀ᶜ(1) = ε for the L²-normalised prolate), the window shape (from ε/2), the window width (Table 2 of Liang et al. 2026: 5, 5, 6, 7, 8 for ε = 1e-3 … 1e-5) and mesh_spacing = π r_c / c. The measured force error is typically 1–2 ε and does not decrease further when only the mesh is refined: the prolate window's alias level is fixed in grid units. To reach a target Δ, tighten ε (e.g. ε ≈ Δ/2) instead of the mesh.\nMeasured targeting. tune_electrostatics(st, force; target) finds the cheapest mesh (PME/PPPM) or tolerance (ESP) whose measured error against a high-accuracy ESP reference at the same cutoff meets target. It returns an explicit descriptor, so nothing is retuned silently on restart.\nFastest setting. tune_electrostatics(st, force; target, objective=:time) searches more knobs at the same real-space cutoff:\nfor PME/PPPM, the order (4–6 / 5–7), the Ewald α (real-space tail target × 0.01 … 0.3) and the mesh;\nfor ESP, ε and the window width.\nIt measures each candidate through the state's own force evaluation (vdW term and nonbonded backend included) and returns the fastest one that meets target, with the full candidate table.\nThe Float32 reference is an order-6 PME on a fine grid. Float32 ESP references at very tight ε are not accurate enough, because wide prolate windows amplify single-precision rounding.\n\nMeasured accuracy against an independent converged Ewald sum (Float64, 120 random charges, r_c = 4):\n\nMethod Settings relative force RMS\nPME α = 0.9, P = 6, h ≈ 0.30 1.3e-5\nPME α = 0.9, P = 8, h ≈ 0.22 < 1e-6\nPPPM-AD α = 0.9, P = 6, h ≈ 0.30 1.3e-5\nPPPM-ik α = 0.9, P = 6, h ≈ 0.30 5.9e-7\nESP ε = 1e-6, P = 8, h ≈ 0.45 3.4e-6\n\nFloat32 adds roughly 1e-6 to 1e-5 on top of the discretisation error.\n\nCross-engine agreement on a 1000-ion snapshot (r_c = 1 nm, same Coulomb constant): against GROMACS 2025 double-precision PME (order 10, 72³ grid) the PME energy agrees to 6e-13 relative, PPPM-AD/ik to 8e-11 and ESP to 4e-10. Forces agree to the 6-digit precision of the GROMACS trajectory dump. Against LAMMPS pppm 1e-9, all three methods differ by the same 1.6e-7 (energy) and 6e-7 (forces), which is LAMMPS's own discretisation error at that setting.","title":"Choosing parameters"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/electrostatics/#Performance","page":"Electrostatics","text":"scripts/bench_electrostatics.jl times the solvers at matched measured force error. It uses a dense ±1 e ion melt in Å/eV units, Coulomb only, r_c = 9 Å, Float32, an RTX 3090 and spatially ordered storage; each entry is one force call:\n\nN Δ SPME P3M-AD P3M-ik ESP\n64 000 1e-4 1.14 ms (150³) 1.09 ms (150³) 1.15 ms (120³) 0.74 ms (50³)\n64 000 1e-5 12.5 ms (375³) 12.5 ms (375³) 6.7 ms (270³) 1.6 ms (63³)\n512 000 1e-4 13.5 ms (300³) 13.5 ms (300³) 14.0 ms (240³) 7.6 ms (100³)\n512 000 1e-5 mesh > 400³ mesh > 400³ mesh > 400³ 13.5 ms (125³)\n\nESP's advantage comes from its much smaller FFT grid and grows with the accuracy target. Spreading and gathering walk particles in the cell list's spatial order, so they stay efficient for any storage order. The real-space kernel profits from spatial reordering (NVE), like the vdW kernels.","title":"Performance"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/electrostatics/#Historical-component-timings-with-other-engines","page":"Electrostatics","text":"The test is equilibrated SPC/E water (the series of Liang et al. 2026) with O–O Lennard-Jones and Coulomb at rc = 1 nm, on one RTX 3090. Every engine runs in its production precision and at a measured accuracy target: relative RMS Coulomb force error Δ against a Float64 reference, checked on a held-out configuration. ParticleDynamics uses the cluster-pair backend with the setting returned by `tuneelectrostatics(...; objective=:time)`. Times are milliseconds.\n\nParticleDynamics: one complete force evaluation (LJ + Coulomb).\nGROMACS 2026.2: one GPU-resident MD step (-update gpu), production Verlet buffer. In brackets, the step minus its CPU pair search.\nOpenMM 8.5.1: one CUDA Verlet step (force + integration).\n\nN Δ GROMACS OpenMM PD PME PD PPPM-AD PD ESP\n24 000 1e-3 0.160 (0.115) 0.215 0.191 0.229 0.216\n24 000 1e-4 0.196 (0.137) 0.272 0.230 0.238 0.326\n192 000 1e-3 1.14 (0.81) 1.54 0.98 1.04 0.93\n192 000 1e-4 1.27 (0.96) 2.24 1.09 1.17 1.06\n648 000 1e-3 3.92 (2.72) 5.14 2.69 2.87 2.33\n648 000 1e-4 4.38 (3.20) 10.1 3.51 3.27 3.03\n1 029 000 1e-3 6.24 (4.37) 9.58 4.21 4.48 3.69\n1 029 000 1e-4 7.31 (5.44) 15.5 6.07 6.01 4.86\n\nLAMMPS 2026 (KOKKOS/CUDA, mixed precision, pppm) takes 25.2 ms (Δ = 1e-3) and 52.7 ms (1e-4) at 1M atoms, against 5.5 and 11.2 ms for PD PPPM-ik.\n\nThese historical rows measure different amounts of work. The PD values exclude integration, coupling, list rebuilds and re-pruning, while the other engines time simulation steps. Subtracting GROMACS's CPU pair-search time does not make the work equivalent. The table describes component costs; it does not establish a complete simulation speedup or equilibrium accuracy. Use the complete-step benchmark suites with matched observation schedules, validated list policies and ensemble checks for those comparisons.\n\nbenchmark/electrostatics/ holds the harness (run_water.jl, run_tuned.jl, run_openmm.jl) and its method notes.","title":"Historical component timings with other engines"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/electrostatics/#Low-level-API","page":"Electrostatics","text":"st = build_simulation(N=N, box=(L, L, L), nonbonded=:none, cutoff=rc,\n                      precision=:f64, charges=q)\ncopyto!(st.rx, x); copyto!(st.ry, y); copyto!(st.rz, z)\nattach_electrostatics!(st, PME(coulomb_prefactor=1.0, real_cutoff=rc, alpha=1.0,\n                               mesh=(32, 32, 32)); exceptions=[(1, 2, 0.0)])\nSimulationCore.evaluate_forces_into_f!(st, true)\nelectrostatic_energy(st)\n\nbuild_simulation(...; neighbor_cutoff=rc) widens the neighbor list of a vdW state to the Coulomb cutoff; attach_electrostatics! also widens a dense list automatically.","title":"Low-level API"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/electrostatics/#Provenance","page":"Electrostatics","text":"The ESP implementation follows the mathematics published by Liang, Lu, Barnett, Greengard and Jiang, Accelerating molecular dynamics simulations using fast Ewald summation with prolates, Nat. Commun. 17, 6681 (2026). The prolate functions are computed from the Legendre expansion of the prolate differential operator. The split and window shapes follow the paper's rule ψ₀ᶜ(1) = ε, and Table 2 is reproduced as a test. No source code or coefficient tables from the published reference implementations were used. References: Essmann et al., J. Chem. Phys. 103, 8577 (1995) (SPME); Hockney and Eastwood (1988) and Deserno and Holm, J. Chem. Phys. 109, 7678 (1998) (P3M); Ballenegger, Cerdà and Holm, J. Chem. Theory Comput. 8, 936 (2012) (influence functions for AD/ik).","title":"Provenance"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/electrostatics/#ParticleDynamics.Workflow.PME","page":"Electrostatics","text":"PME(; coulomb_prefactor, real_cutoff, alpha=nothing, real_space_tolerance=nothing,\n      mesh=nothing, mesh_spacing=nothing, order=4, relative_permittivity=1.0,\n      net_charge=:error, boundary=:tin_foil, real_space_modifier=:none,\n      exclusions=:topology, exclude_bonded=false, scaled_pairs=(),\n      pairs=:neighborlist, neighborlist=nothing)\n\nSmooth particle-mesh Ewald (Essmann et al. 1995) with analytic differentiation, computed entirely on the GPU.\n\nUnits are the caller's: U = C qᵢqⱼ/r with C = coulomb_prefactor / relative_permittivity, so coulomb_prefactor carries E·L/Q² (≈138.935458 for nm/kJ mol⁻¹/e, ≈14.399645 for Å/eV/e, or 1 in reduced units). alpha is in inverse length units; alternatively real_space_tolerance sets it from erfc(α r_c) = tol (a real-space potential tail criterion, not a force error). mesh=(K₁,K₂,K₃) or mesh_spacing (FFT friendly sizes are chosen) and the B-spline order (stencil width) set the reciprocal accuracy.\n\nExceptions: exclusions=:topology excludes Topology.exclusions pairs (scale 0); exclude_bonded=true also excludes bonded pairs; scaled_pairs lists (i, j, scale) triples (e.g. 1–4 scaling) and overrides the other two. Each exception removes or scales the pair's minimum-image interaction while its periodic images remain.\n\nnet_charge=:error rejects charged systems; :background adds a uniform neutralising background (energy and virial, no force).\n\nreal_space_modifier=:potential_shift subtracts the short-range pair energy at the cutoff from every non-excluded pair inside it (energy only; forces are unchanged), matching the GROMACS coulomb-modifier = Potential-shift Hamiltonian; the default :none is the unshifted Ewald sum.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Workflow.PME"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/electrostatics/#ParticleDynamics.Workflow.PPPM","page":"Electrostatics","text":"PPPM(; coulomb_prefactor, real_cutoff, alpha=nothing, real_space_tolerance=nothing,\n       mesh=nothing, mesh_spacing=nothing, order=5, differentiation=:ad,\n       alias_order=2, kwargs...)\n\nParticle–particle particle–mesh (P3M) with the Hockney–Eastwood optimal influence function for B-spline assignment. differentiation=:ad uses analytic gradients of the assignment weights (one forward and one inverse transform, influence function optimised for AD); :ik forms the electric field in reciprocal space (one forward and three inverse transforms). Alias sums run over |n| ≤ alias_order images per dimension. All other keywords match PME.\n\nThe ik forces are not the exact gradient of the reported mesh energy; the energy and virial are those of the discrete influence function.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Workflow.PPPM"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/electrostatics/#ParticleDynamics.Workflow.ESP","page":"Electrostatics","text":"ESP(; coulomb_prefactor, real_cutoff, tolerance=1e-4, split_shape=nothing,\n      window_shape=nothing, order=nothing, mesh=nothing, mesh_spacing=nothing,\n      kwargs...)\n\nEwald summation with prolates (Liang, Lu, Barnett, Greengard & Jiang 2026): the Gaussian split is replaced by one built from the prolate spheroidal wave function ψ₀ᶜ, and B-spline assignment by a prolate window of width order grid points. The real-space part vanishes exactly at real_cutoff.\n\nDefaults follow the paper's selection rules for a target relative force error tolerance ε: split_shape c with ψ₀ᶜ(1) = ε (L²-normalised), window_shape from ε/2, order from Table 2 of the paper (5, 5, 6, 7, 8 for ε = 1e-3, 5e-4, 1e-4, 5e-5, 1e-5), and mesh_spacing = π r_c / c. All dimensionless shapes are distinct from the Gaussian alpha (inverse length). Treat the defaults as starting points and measure the error for production settings.\n\ninfluence=:optimal (default) uses the analytic-differentiation optimal influence function with alias sums over |n| ≤ alias_order images of the prolate window transform, which compensates the aliasing of the truncated window. influence=:deconvolution uses the plain window deconvolution ĝ/φ̂² of the paper's equation (9). The remaining keywords match PME (alpha/ real_space_tolerance do not apply).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Workflow.ESP"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/electrostatics/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.attach_electrostatics!","page":"Electrostatics","text":"attach_electrostatics!(st, spec::Electrostatics.ElectrostaticsSpec;\n                       charges=nothing, exceptions=()) -> st\n\nAttach a long-range Coulomb solver to a 3D state. charges (build-time particle order, caller's charge unit) replace st.charge; when omitted the state must already carry charges. exceptions lists (i, j, scale) triples in build-time ids: the pair's home-image interaction C qᵢqⱼ/r is scaled by scale (0 excludes it) while periodic images keep the full interaction.\n\nSetup validates the box (real_cutoff below half the shortest box length, orthorhombic 3D), plans cuFFT transforms and computes the influence function. The state moves to the cluster-pair backend (charged systems), which keeps its storage order unsorted.\n\n\n\n\n\nattach_electrostatics!(st, force::ElectrostaticsForce; charges=nothing, exceptions=())\n\nLow-level convenience: attach the solver described by a workflow descriptor to an existing state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.attach_electrostatics!"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/electrostatics/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.detach_electrostatics!","page":"Electrostatics","text":"detach_electrostatics!(st) -> st\n\nRemove the long-range solver (charges stay stored) and clear the global virial contribution.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.detach_electrostatics!"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/electrostatics/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.set_charges!","page":"Electrostatics","text":"set_charges!(st, q) -> st\n\nReplace the per-particle charges (q in build-time particle order). With an attached solver the net-charge policy is re-checked and the background term refreshed. Forces are invalidated.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.set_charges!"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/electrostatics/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.charges","page":"Electrostatics","text":"charges(st) -> Vector or nothing\n\nHost copy of the charges in build-time particle order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.charges"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/electrostatics/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.electrostatic_energy","page":"Electrostatics","text":"electrostatic_energy(st) -> NamedTuple\n\nDiagnostic breakdown of the electrostatic energy at the current coordinates: real (real-space pairs plus exception corrections), self, reciprocal (mesh), background and total, plus the reciprocal virial tensor reciprocal_virial (xx, yy, zz, xy, xz, yz). Uses scratch buffers; the state's force and energy buffers are untouched. Not for the hot path.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.electrostatic_energy"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/electrostatics/#ParticleDynamics.Workflow.electrostatics_from_gsd","page":"Electrostatics","text":"electrostatics_from_gsd(path; frame=-1) -> ElectrostaticsForce or nothing\n\nRead the solver settings and exceptions stored in a GSD frame written while electrostatics was attached, and return the matching descriptor (nothing if the frame carries no electrostatics record).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Workflow.electrostatics_from_gsd"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/electrostatics/#ParticleDynamics.Workflow.tune_electrostatics","page":"Electrostatics","text":"tune_electrostatics(st, force; target, reference_tolerance=target/1000,\n                    max_mesh=512, spacings=nothing) -> NamedTuple\n\nChoose the cheapest accuracy setting of force that meets a measured relative RMS force error target on the charges and coordinates of st (a state whose neighbor list covers force.real_cutoff).\n\nWith objective=:time the search is over every knob that keeps the real-space cutoff, and the fastest complete force evaluation of st (its own vdW term and nonbonded backend included) that meets target wins:\n\nPME and PPPM: assignment order (orders, default 4–6 for PME and 5–7 for PPPM), the Ewald α through the real-space tail (real_space_tolerances, default target × (0.01, 0.03, 0.1, 0.3)) and the coarsest passing mesh spacing for each pair;\nESP: the tolerance ε and the window width (default width and one point narrower or wider, within the shape's bound).\n\nThe error is measured on the Coulomb forces through the state's force path (forces without electrostatics are subtracted), against an ESP reference at reference_tolerance (Float64 states) or an order-6 PME on a grid of spacing rc/14 (Float32 states, ~5e-6 accurate). The state's solver is restored afterwards. Returns `(force, error, timems, mesh, candidates), wherecandidates` lists every passing setting with its error and time.\n\nThe reference is an ESP solve at the same cutoff with prolate tolerance reference_tolerance; ESP converges at fixed cutoff, so it reuses the state's neighbor list. For PME and PPPM the mesh spacing is refined (cutoff, alpha/real_space_tolerance and order are kept). For ESP the tolerance ε is tightened with the paper's rules for shape, width and spacing; refining an ESP mesh alone saturates near ε.\n\nReturns (force, error, time_ms, mesh, reference_error_bound). force is an explicit descriptor (fixed mesh, no hidden retuning), error the measured error against the reference, time_ms the median force-call time and reference_error_bound the reference's own measured change when ε is halved. Electrostatic exceptions are not included in the comparison (they are identical for every candidate).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Workflow.tune_electrostatics"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/electrostatics/#ParticleDynamics.Electrostatics.ElectrostaticsSpec","page":"Electrostatics","text":"ElectrostaticsSpec\n\nValidated, host-side description of a long-range Coulomb solver. Built by the workflow descriptors PME, PPPM and ESP; only the combinations those constructors produce are supported:\n\ninfluence split window differentiation\n:spme Gaussian B-spline analytic\n:p3m_ad Gaussian B-spline analytic, optimal AD influence\n:p3m_ik Gaussian B-spline ik (three field transforms)\n:esp prolate prolate analytic, optimal AD influence (alias sums)\n:esp_deconvolution prolate prolate analytic, plain window deconvolution\n\nFields: coulomb_prefactor, relative_permittivity (C = prefactor/εᵣ), real_cutoff, split, window, mesh ((K₁,K₂,K₃)) or mesh_spacing, influence, alias_order (P3M alias sums), net_charge (:error or :background), boundary (:tin_foil), and real_space_modifier: :none (unshifted), or :potential_shift, which subtracts the short-range pair energy at the cutoff from every non-excluded pair inside it (energy only, forces unchanged; the GROMACS Potential-shift convention). Prolate splits vanish at the cutoff, so the shift is zero for ESP.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Electrostatics.ElectrostaticsSpec"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/groups_filters/#Groups-and-Filters","page":"Groups and Filters","text":"Particle groups are the stable public selection API. The exported Filters module layers higher-level workflows on top of those selections.","title":"Groups and Filters"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/groups_filters/#Stable-selection-API","page":"Groups and Filters","text":"","title":"Stable selection API"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/groups_filters/#Typical-uses","page":"Groups and Filters","text":"define thermostat baths by particle subset\nassign temperatures or frictions to selected particles\ngather subset observables without copying the whole state to host\nfreeze/tether subsets through the higher-level Filters workflows","title":"Typical uses"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/groups_filters/#Notes","page":"Groups and Filters","text":"ParticleGroup is the reusable materialized form.\nThe top-level stable API is the group layer above raw implementation buffers.\nThe Filters module remains the place for freeze controls and stochastic selector-based convenience operations.","title":"Notes"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/groups_filters/#Example-scripts","page":"Groups and Filters","text":"examples/TwoT_2D_LD_BAOAB.jl\nexamples/TwoT_SR2D_MD_CSVR_slab.jl\nexamples/TwoT_2D_LD_freeze_hold.jl\nexamples/TwoT_2D_LD_freeze_spring.jl\n\nThese examples are GPU-first today. Small subset-control cases should become good CPU-compatible smoke candidates only after a CPU execution backend exists.","title":"Example scripts"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/groups_filters/#ParticleDynamics.ParticleGroups.ParticleSelection","page":"Groups and Filters","text":"Abstract base for particle selection specifications.\n\nConcrete subtypes include All, TypeIDs, Indices, and can be extended by users for custom selection logic.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.ParticleGroups.ParticleSelection"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/groups_filters/#ParticleDynamics.ParticleGroups.ParticleGroup","page":"Groups and Filters","text":"ParticleGroup\n\nMaterialized particle selection containing both host and device indices. Reusable across multiple operations without recomputation.\n\nInstances are created via materialize(selection, state).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.ParticleGroups.ParticleGroup"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/groups_filters/#ParticleDynamics.ParticleGroups.All","page":"Groups and Filters","text":"Select all particles.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.ParticleGroups.All"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/groups_filters/#ParticleDynamics.ParticleGroups.TypeIDs","page":"Groups and Filters","text":"Select particles by type ID. Accepts a single Int or a vector.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.ParticleGroups.TypeIDs"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/groups_filters/#ParticleDynamics.ParticleGroups.Indices","page":"Groups and Filters","text":"Select particles by explicit list of indices.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.ParticleGroups.Indices"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/groups_filters/#ParticleDynamics.ParticleGroups.materialize","page":"Groups and Filters","text":"materialize(sel::ParticleSelection, state) -> ParticleGroup\n\nCreate a reusable materialized group from a selection and state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.ParticleGroups.materialize"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/groups_filters/#ParticleDynamics.ParticleGroups.apply_scalar!","page":"Groups and Filters","text":"apply_scalar!(dest::CuArray, group::ParticleGroup, value)\n\nAssign value to all elements of dest indexed by group.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.ParticleGroups.apply_scalar!"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/groups_filters/#ParticleDynamics.ParticleGroups.apply_values!","page":"Groups and Filters","text":"apply_values!(dest::CuArray, group::ParticleGroup, values)\n\nAssign distinct values per group member.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.ParticleGroups.apply_values!"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/groups_filters/#ParticleDynamics.ParticleGroups.gather","page":"Groups and Filters","text":"gather(src::CuArray, group::ParticleGroup) -> Vector\n\nCollect values from device array indexed by group onto the host.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.ParticleGroups.gather"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/groups_filters/#ParticleDynamics.ParticleGroups.sum_values","page":"Groups and Filters","text":"sum_values(src::CuArray, group::ParticleGroup) -> Scalar\n\nSum values from device array indexed by group.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.ParticleGroups.sum_values"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/io/#I/O","page":"I/O","text":"Writers and trajectory helpers are kept at the top level so user scripts can write diagnostics without importing internal modules.","title":"I/O"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/io/#Public-writer-API","page":"I/O","text":"","title":"Public writer API"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/io/#GSD-metadata-types","page":"I/O","text":"","title":"GSD metadata types"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/io/#Notes","page":"I/O","text":"Workflow TableWriter and GSDWriter use mode=:replace to replace a file once when their simulation writer session first opens it. Later stages append to that session, even though handles are closed at stage boundaries. Calling prepare! again with the same writer preserves the session. A new Simulation starts a new session; use mode=:append to retain an existing file across separate simulations.\nXYZ and CSV helpers stage data to host for output.\nGSD writing stays outside the simulation hot path and is the preferred trajectory format for richer restart data.","title":"Notes"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/io/#Example-scripts","page":"I/O","text":"examples/3D_quicktest.jl\nexamples/2D_allpairs_quicktest.jl\n\nThese remain GPU-first examples. The small quicktests are the best future CPU-compatible candidates once a CPU backend becomes real.","title":"Example scripts"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/io/#ParticleDynamics.Writers.InMemoryLogger","page":"I/O","text":"Lightweight logger that records selected observables in host memory every every steps. Useful for quick ad-hoc diagnostics during testing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Writers.InMemoryLogger"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/io/#ParticleDynamics.Writers.CSVWriter","page":"I/O","text":"Stream particle tables to CSV (one row per particle). Mirrors the logging used in the earlier examples/ scripts.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Writers.CSVWriter"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/io/#ParticleDynamics.Writers.XYZWriter","page":"I/O","text":"Minimal XYZ trajectory writer. Each call to write! appends one frame; z is set to zero for 2D states, matching the usage in examples/2D_example.jl.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Writers.XYZWriter"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/io/#ParticleDynamics.Writers.write_xyz!","page":"I/O","text":"write_xyz!(path; rx, ry[, rz], atomsym=\"A\")\n\nWrite a single XYZ frame without constructing an XYZWriter. Mirrors the ad-hoc dumping performed in examples/2D_example.jl.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Writers.write_xyz!"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/io/#ParticleDynamics.Writers.write_observables_csv!","page":"I/O","text":"write_observables_csv!(path, step; Epot, Ekin, dq)\n\nAppend a single line with step, Etot, Kavg, Qtot (mirrors examples/2D_example.jl and the 3D variants).\n\n\n\n\n\nwrite_observables_csv!(path, st, spec)\n\nWrite observables collected through SimulationCore.collect_step_observables, including a compatibility Qtot column.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Writers.write_observables_csv!"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/io/#ParticleDynamics.Writers.gsd_open","page":"I/O","text":"gsd_open(f::Function, path; kwargs...)\n\nConvenience do-block form that guarantees the GSD handle is closed, even if an exception occurs during writing. Example:\n\nWriters.gsd_open(\"traj.gsd\") do h\n    for step in 1:ns\n        Writers.write_gsd_frame!(h, st; step)\n    end\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Writers.gsd_open"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/io/#ParticleDynamics.Writers.gsd_close","page":"I/O","text":"Close a previously opened GSD handle.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Writers.gsd_close"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/io/#ParticleDynamics.Writers.write_gsd_frame!","page":"I/O","text":"write_gsd_frame!(h, st; diameter=nothing, mass=nothing, charge=nothing,\n                  types_names=[\"A\"], step=0,\n                  write_forces=false, write_unwrapped=false,\n                  write_virial=false, sync_on_write=false)\n\nWrite one frame of st to the open GSD handle h at the given step.\n\nPositions, and (unless the last integrator step was position-only) velocities, are always written. diameter, mass, and charge accept nothing (use GSD defaults), a scalar, or a per-particle-or-per-type vector (expanded from per-type values via types_names). With charge=nothing, a charged state writes st.charge. While a long-range electrostatics solver is attached, the frame also records its exact settings (log/electrostatics/settings) and exception list (log/electrostatics/exceptions) for restarts; see electrostatics_from_gsd. Set write_forces, write_unwrapped, or write_virial to additionally store forces, unwrapped positions (requires unwrapped_positions=true in build_simulation), or the virial tensor. Output is written in build-time particle order regardless of any internal spatial reordering.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Writers.write_gsd_frame!"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/io/#ParticleDynamics.Writers.GSDFrameData","page":"I/O","text":"Container returned by read_gsd_frame!.\n\nBehaves like the legacy tuple (step, rx, ry, rz, vx, vy, vz, typeid, types, box, force) when destructured/iterated, while exposing richer metadata via fields:\n\nstep, N, D, box: configuration metadata.\nrx, ry, rz, vx, vy, vz: particle state vectors (host arrays).\ntypeid, types: 1-based ids and type names.\nforceM: optional force matrix (N×3, same precision as positions).\nparticle_properties: dictionary with optional particle properties (mass, charge, diameter, body, orientation, etc) converted to sensible Julia arrays. When present, custom force/virial chunks are also exposed as :force and :virial; other particles/property/* chunks appear under the :property key.\nper_type_properties: dictionary for particles/type_* chunks (e.g. :shapes for per-type shape JSON blobs).\ntopology: a GSDTopology with bonds/angles/… converted to 1-based indices.\nconfiguration: hierarchical NamedTuple combining metadata, particle data, topology and any extra configuration chunks for convenience.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Writers.GSDFrameData"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/io/#ParticleDynamics.Writers.GSDTopology","page":"I/O","text":"Structured toplogical information decoded from a GSD frame.\n\nEach field is a NamedTuple describing the corresponding collection and contains both the original 0-based data (group0, typeid0) and the converted 1-based indices (group, typeid) that can be passed directly to ParticleDynamics utilities such as BondedForces.build_bondlist.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Writers.GSDTopology"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/integrators/#Integrators","page":"Integrators","text":"The package exposes explicit integrator constructors that allocate and own the workspace needed for repeated stepping. Reuse a spec across many step! calls.","title":"Integrators"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/integrators/#Integrator-constructors","page":"Integrators","text":"","title":"Integrator constructors"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/integrators/#Public-spec-types","page":"Integrators","text":"","title":"Public spec types"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/integrators/#Usage-pattern","page":"Integrators","text":"nve_spec = nve(st; dt=2.0f-4)\nfor _ in 1:100\n    step!(st, nve_spec, 2.0f-4; compute_energy=false)\nend","title":"Usage pattern"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/integrators/#Timestep-consistency","page":"Integrators","text":"step! and step_graph! require a finite positive timestep. For Langevin and Brownian specs, it must equal the timestep supplied to their constructor (after conversion to the simulation precision). Noise amplitudes and OU coefficients are cached for that timestep. A mismatch throws ArgumentError before stepping changes state or consumes random numbers. The low-level EM and Langevin wrappers that consume cached stochastic parameters enforce the same contract. For static forces and virials, use ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.evaluate_forces_into_f!(st, true); zero-duration steps are rejected.\n\ndt = 0.001f0\nspec = eulermaruyama(st; gamma=1, temperature=1, dt=dt)\nstep!(st, spec, dt)\n\nTo change the timestep, construct a new spec with all desired friction, temperature, and OU settings. This starts a new stochastic workspace; it does not preserve the previous OU realization. Temperature and OU setters require the existing spec's timestep, including filtered updates. Raw parameter constructors that omit dt default to one; pass dt explicitly when wrapping these parameters in a spec or passing them to step!.\n\nFilters.set_friction! controls friction independently of the noise amplitude. To maintain a chosen thermal temperature after changing friction, also call Filters.set_temperature! for the same particles and the spec's timestep.","title":"Timestep consistency"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/integrators/#Notes","page":"Integrators","text":"nve is the deterministic microcanonical MD path.\nLangevin and Brownian constructors own stochastic buffers.\ngamma > 0 is required on stochastic paths that divide by friction.\nThermostat-driven MD paths (nosehooverchain, csvr) allocate device workspaces per bath and remain CUDA-specific internally.","title":"Notes"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/integrators/#Example-scripts","page":"Integrators","text":"examples/2D_example.jl\nexamples/TwoT_2D_LD_BAOAB.jl\nexamples/TwoT_2D_LD_GSM.jl\nexamples/TwoT_2D_BD_EH.jl\nexamples/3D_BD.jl\nexamples/3D_LJ_NVE.jl","title":"Example scripts"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/integrators/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.nve","page":"Integrators","text":"nve(st; dt=st.dt) -> NVESpec\n\nCreate a deterministic microcanonical MD integrator using plain velocity Verlet. Unlike the stochastic velocityverlet constructor, this path owns no thermostat or noise parameters and advances only under conservative forces.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.nve"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/integrators/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.velocityverlet","page":"Integrators","text":"velocityverlet(st; gamma, temperature, noise_corr_time=nothing, ou_scales=nothing,\n               mass=st.mass, dt=st.dt) -> VVSpec\n\nConstruct a GJF/Langevin velocity-Verlet spec with integrator-owned stochastic buffers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.velocityverlet"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/integrators/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.baoab","page":"Integrators","text":"baoab(st; gamma, temperature, noise_corr_time=nothing, ou_scales=nothing,\n      mass=st.mass, dt=st.dt) -> BAOABSpec\n\nConstruct a BAOAB Langevin spec with integrator-owned stochastic buffers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.baoab"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/integrators/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.baoa","page":"Integrators","text":"baoa(st; gamma, temperature, noise_corr_time=nothing, ou_scales=nothing,\n     mass=st.mass, dt=st.dt) -> BAOASpec\n\nLegacy BAOA variant (no final B kick).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.baoa"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/integrators/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.gsm","page":"Integrators","text":"gsm(st; gamma, temperature, noise_corr_time=nothing, ou_scales=nothing,\n    mass=st.mass, dt=st.dt) -> GSMSpec\n\nConstruct a GSM spec (used by examples/TwoT_2D_LD_GSM.jl).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.gsm"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/integrators/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.eulerheun","page":"Integrators","text":"eulerheun(st; gamma, temperature, noise_corr_time=nothing, ou_scales=nothing,\n          dt=st.dt) -> BrownianSpec\n\nBuild a midpoint Brownian spec with integrator-owned stochastic buffers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.eulerheun"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/integrators/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.eulermaruyama","page":"Integrators","text":"eulermaruyama(st; gamma, temperature, noise_corr_time=nothing, ou_scales=nothing,\n              dt=st.dt) -> EMSpec\n\nReturn an Euler-Maruyama spec for overdamped dynamics (examples/3D_BD.jl).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.eulermaruyama"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/integrators/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.nosehooverchain","page":"Integrators","text":"nosehooverchain(st; temperature=1, tau=1, chain_length=5, substeps=5,\n                mass=st.mass, chain_masses=nothing, propagator=:gromacs) -> NHCSpec\n\nCreate a deterministic NVT integrator specification using a Nose-Hoover Chain. When chain_masses is omitted, masses are initialized from (dof, T, tau) using the standard Q₁ = g T τ², Qⱼ = T τ² (j>1) rule. propagator selects the chain update scheme: :gromacs is the package default and uses a GPU port of the reversible Suzuki-Yoshida propagator used by GROMACS, :lammps uses a GPU port of the reversible tloop chain update used by LAMMPS, and :legacy preserves the original package implementation. For propagator=:gromacs, substeps=5 matches the default fifth-order outer repetition used there; for propagator=:lammps, substeps corresponds to LAMMPS tloop and substeps=1 matches the LAMMPS default.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.nosehooverchain"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/integrators/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.csvr","page":"Integrators","text":"csvr(st; temperature=1, tau=1, mass=st.mass, interval=1) -> CSVRSpec\ncsvr(st; temperatures, taus, mass=st.mass, interval=1) -> CSVRSpec\n\nCreate a deterministic MD integrator using the Bussi canonical-sampling through velocity rescaling (CSVR) thermostat. The thermostat acts on one or more baths defined by filter assignments, with one global velocity-rescaling factor drawn per bath every interval steps (GROMACS nsttcouple), each coupling integrating the thermostat over interval·dt.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.csvr"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/integrators/#ParticleDynamics.IntegratorInterfaces.AbstractIntegratorSpec","page":"Integrators","text":"AbstractIntegratorSpec{T}\n\nBase type of the integrator specs (VV, BAOAB, BAOA, GSM, Brownian, EM, NHC, CSVR, NVE). The device engine (ParticleDynamics.DeviceEngine) steps every spec in captured CUDA graphs; the protocol here validates inputs and sizes per-spec workspaces.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.IntegratorInterfaces.AbstractIntegratorSpec"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/integrators/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.VVSpec","page":"Integrators","text":"Concrete spec for the package's Langevin/GJF velocityverlet path.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.VVSpec"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/integrators/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.BAOABSpec","page":"Integrators","text":"BAOAB splitting spec used in examples/TwoT_2D_LD_BAOAB.jl.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.BAOABSpec"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/integrators/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.BAOASpec","page":"Integrators","text":"BAOA splitting (no trailing B) for legacy scripts.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.BAOASpec"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/integrators/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.GSMSpec","page":"Integrators","text":"Generalized simulation scheme (GSM) spec, which reuses the BAOAB parameter type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.GSMSpec"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/integrators/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.BrownianSpec","page":"Integrators","text":"Midpoint Brownian integrator spec created by eulerheun.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.BrownianSpec"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/integrators/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.EMSpec","page":"Integrators","text":"Euler-Maruyama overdamped spec created by eulermaruyama.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.EMSpec"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/integrators/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.NVESpec","page":"Integrators","text":"NVESpec{T}\n\nConcrete integrator specification for deterministic microcanonical (NVE) dynamics using plain velocity Verlet.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.NVESpec"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/integrators/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.NHCSpec","page":"Integrators","text":"NHCSpec{T}\n\nConcrete integrator specification for NVT dynamics with a Nose-Hoover Chain thermostat.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.NHCSpec"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/integrators/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.CSVRSpec","page":"Integrators","text":"CSVRSpec{T}\n\nConcrete integrator specification for deterministic NVT dynamics with a stochastic global Bussi/CSVR thermostat.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.CSVRSpec"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/thermostats/#Thermostats","page":"Thermostats","text":"Thermostat objects describe bath structure and expose state/parameter accessors that can be used from diagnostics or higher-level workflows.","title":"Thermostats"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/thermostats/#Public-thermostat-API","page":"Thermostats","text":"","title":"Public thermostat API"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/thermostats/#Runtime-construction","page":"Thermostats","text":"Thermostat-backed integrator specs are built through:\n\nnosehooverchain(st; ...)\ncsvr(st; ...)\n\nThose constructors remain the recommended entrypoints for stepping.","title":"Runtime construction"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/thermostats/#Example-scripts","page":"Thermostats","text":"examples/TwoT_SR2D_MD_CSVR_slab.jl\nexamples/SingleT_2D_MD_CSVR.jl\nexamples/TwoT_LJ2D_MD_NHC.jl\n\nThe thermostat examples remain GPU-first. Bath-control logic is a likely later candidate for CPU-compatible validation only after kernel portability work.","title":"Example scripts"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/thermostats/#ParticleDynamics.Thermostats.AbstractThermostat","page":"Thermostats","text":"Abstract base type for all thermostat implementations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Thermostats.AbstractThermostat"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/thermostats/#ParticleDynamics.Thermostats.ThermostatState","page":"Thermostats","text":"Abstract base for thermostat state containers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Thermostats.ThermostatState"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/thermostats/#ParticleDynamics.Thermostats.NoseHooverChainThermostat","page":"Thermostats","text":"NoseHooverChainThermostat{T} <: AbstractThermostat\n\nDeterministic Nose-Hoover Chain thermostat with multi-bath support.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Thermostats.NoseHooverChainThermostat"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/thermostats/#ParticleDynamics.Thermostats.CSVRThermostat","page":"Thermostats","text":"CSVRThermostat{T} <: AbstractThermostat\n\nStochastic CSVR/Bussi velocity-rescaling thermostat with multi-bath support.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Thermostats.CSVRThermostat"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/thermostats/#ParticleDynamics.Thermostats.n_baths","page":"Thermostats","text":"n_baths(thermo::AbstractThermostat) -> Int\n\nNumber of baths in this thermostat.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Thermostats.n_baths"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/thermostats/#ParticleDynamics.Thermostats.target_temperature","page":"Thermostats","text":"target_temperature(thermo::AbstractThermostat) -> Vector\n\nPer-bath target temperatures.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Thermostats.target_temperature"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/thermostats/#ParticleDynamics.Thermostats.response_time","page":"Thermostats","text":"response_time(thermo::AbstractThermostat) -> Vector\n\nPer-bath response times (tau).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Thermostats.response_time"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/thermostats/#ParticleDynamics.Thermostats.set_target_temperature!","page":"Thermostats","text":"set_target_temperature!(thermo::AbstractThermostat, T_new)\n\nUpdate target temperature(s).\n\nAccepts:\n\nScalar: apply to all baths\nVector: length must match n_baths(thermo)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Thermostats.set_target_temperature!"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/thermostats/#ParticleDynamics.Thermostats.set_response_time!","page":"Thermostats","text":"set_response_time!(thermo::AbstractThermostat, tau_new)\n\nUpdate response time(s).\n\nAccepts:\n\nScalar: apply to all baths\nVector: length must match n_baths(thermo)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Thermostats.set_response_time!"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/thermostats/#ParticleDynamics.Thermostats.cumulative_energy_exchange","page":"Thermostats","text":"cumulative_energy_exchange(thermo::AbstractThermostat) -> CuArray{T}\n\nCumulative energy exchanged with the thermostat reservoir per bath.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Thermostats.cumulative_energy_exchange"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/bonded_minimization_npt/#Bonded-Terms,-Minimization-and-NPT","page":"Bonded, Minimization, NPT","text":"All quantities are plain numbers in caller-chosen consistent units (length L, energy E, time). Angles are radians.","title":"Bonded Terms, Minimization and NPT"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/bonded_minimization_npt/#Typed-bonded-interactions","page":"Bonded, Minimization, NPT","text":"Topology sections carry optional labels; each force descriptor applies to the tuples whose label equals its type (:default when a section has no labels). Atom order is kept: the middle atom of an angle is the vertex, and torsions use the IUPAC signed dihedral φ = atan2(|b2| b1·(b2×b3), (b1×b2)·(b2×b3)) of the ordered quartet.\n\ntopology = Topology(bonds=bonds, bond_types=[:backbone, :side, …],\n                    angles=angles, dihedrals=dihedrals, impropers=impropers)\nforces = Force[\n    WCA(epsilon=1.0, sigma=1.0),\n    HarmonicBondForce(k=300.0, r0=1.0, type=:backbone),\n    FENEBondForce(k=30.0, R0=1.5, type=:side),\n    HarmonicAngleForce(k=30.0, theta0=deg2rad(115)),\n    PeriodicDihedralForce(k=[1.0, 0.4], n=[1, 3], phase=[0.0, 0.0]),\n    HarmonicImproperForce(k=10.0, chi0=0.0),\n]\n\nDescriptor Potential\nHarmonicBondForce(k, r0) k(r-r0)²/2\nFENEBondForce(k, R0) -kR0² log(1-r²/R0²)/2, strictly r < R0\nHarmonicAngleForce(k, theta0) k(θ-θ0)²/2; θ0 = π is exact at collinear geometry\nCosineSquaredAngleForce(k, theta0) k(cosθ-cosθ0)²/2\nUreyBradleyAngleForce(k_theta, theta0, k_r, r13) harmonic angle + k_r(r13-r13⁰)²/2\nPeriodicDihedralForce(k, n, phase) Σ k_m[1+cos(n_mφ-δ_m)]\nOPLSDihedralForce(K1, K2, K3, K4) ½[K1(1+cosφ)+K2(1-cos2φ)+K3(1+cos3φ)+K4(1-cos4φ)]\nRyckaertBellemansDihedralForce(C) Σ Cn cos(ψ)^n, ψ = φ-π\nHarmonicImproperForce(k, chi0) k·wrap(χ-χ0)²/2 (torsion-style, ordered quartet)\nPeriodicImproperForce(k, n, phase) Fourier form on impropers\n\nEvery label must be assigned exactly once per section; missing or duplicate assignments are errors. Torsions and impropers require 3D. Invalid geometry (zero-length bonds, collinear torsion planes, FENE beyond R0) raises DomainError with the topology index. bonded_energy_components(st) returns per-family energies. Exclusion and 1–4 scaling policies beyond the existing bonded-pair exclusion are not implemented yet.","title":"Typed bonded interactions"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/bonded_minimization_npt/#Energy-minimization","page":"Bonded, Minimization, NPT","text":"result = minimize!(sim; method=FIRE(dt_initial=1e-3, dt_max=1e-2),\n                   force_tolerance=1e-4, max_displacement=0.05,\n                   mobile=Group(:solvent, TypeSelection(:W)))\n\nminimize! works on a workflow Simulation (the integrator only supplies forces; no thermostat runs) or on a low-level SimulationState. The box is fixed; non-mobile particles keep their interactions but do not move. FIRE (velocity-Verlet FIRE) uses forces only; SteepestDescent uses a backtracking Armijo line search on energies and therefore stops with :step_too_small once energy differences fall below rounding (in practice about |F| ≈ √(2 k eps E_term)). result.converged is true only when the largest mobile force reached force_tolerance.","title":"Energy minimization"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/bonded_minimization_npt/#Isotropic-NPT","page":"Bonded, Minimization, NPT","text":"barostat = MonteCarloBarostat(pressure=2.0, kT=1.0, interval=25,\n                              log_volume_step=0.01, seed=1, tune=true)\n# or StochasticCellRescaling(pressure=2.0, kT=1.0, compressibility=0.1,\n#                            tau=0.5, interval=10, seed=1)\nintegrator = Integrator(dt=4e-3, scheme=BAOAB(), forces=forces,\n                        methods=[Langevin(all; gamma=1.0, kT=1.0)], barostat=barostat)\n\nPressure is in E/L³, compressibility in L³/E. Both barostats scale all coordinates with the cell and leave velocities unchanged. They currently require 3D, scheme=BAOAB() with one Langevin method on all particles at the barostat kT, no freezing or external potentials. Long-range electrostatics follow the cell: the mesh influence function, reciprocal virial and background term are recomputed at every cell change. Monte Carlo moves use a log-volume proposal with the (N+1) Jacobian; stochastic cell rescaling follows Bernetti & Bussi (2020) with a fresh virial at every event. barostat_diagnostics(sim) reports volume, acceptance and committed changes. GSD frames record log/barostat/state; continue a run with restore_barostat!(sim, read_gsd_log(path, \"log/barostat/state\")).\n\nStates with rigid water (attach_rigid_water!, core API) take Monte Carlo volume moves only. The moves are molecular: set_cell_and_molecules! scales each water's centre of mass and translates the molecule rigidly, and the acceptance counts each water once. C-rescale and atom-wise scaling are rejected for rigid water.\n\nMonte Carlo moves sample the potential energy itself, so the Lennard-Jones cutoff convention matters (vdw_modifier, see Forces). Use :potential_shift to compare with GROMACS.\n\nSize the neighbor-list capacity for the densest volume the run will visit: an overflow stops the run instead of truncating the list. Each barostat event currently triggers a neighbor rebuild, so events every 10–25 steps are a reasonable default.","title":"Isotropic NPT"},{"category":"section","location":"quickstart/#Quickstart","page":"Quickstart","text":"This page shows the recommended public workflow API. It is the interface normal examples and user scripts should use.\n\nParticleDynamics.jl is currently CUDA-first, so the workflow still builds a GPU-resident low-level simulation under the hood. The workflow layer exists to hide manual build_simulation(...), manual stepping loops, manual GSD open/close calls, and manual observable plumbing.","title":"Quickstart"},{"category":"section","location":"quickstart/#Conceptual-model","page":"Quickstart","text":"ParticleSystem: positions, box, types, masses, velocities, topology\nGroup / Groups: particle selections\nForce: physical interactions\nIntegrator: dt, scheme, forces, methods, thermostats\nObservable: sampled quantities\nWriter: scheduled output\nStage: a named block of running\nSimulation: assembled workflow object","title":"Conceptual model"},{"category":"section","location":"quickstart/#Minimal-workflow","page":"Quickstart","text":"using ParticleDynamics\n\nN = 128\ncfg = hex_random_2d(N, 1.0f0, 0.25f0; T=Float32)\n\nsystem = ParticleSystem(\n    cfg;\n    types=[:A],\n    typeids=fill(Int32(1), N),\n    masses=Dict(:A => 1.0f0),\n)\n\nall = Group(:all, AllSelection())\ngroups = Groups(all)\n\nforce = WCA(\n    epsilon=10.0f0,\n    sigma=1.0f0,\n    cutoff=Float32(2^(1 / 6)),\n    pairs=:all,\n)\n\nintegrator = Integrator(\n    dt=2.0f-4,\n    scheme=VelocityVerlet(),\n    forces=[force],\n    methods=[Langevin(all; gamma=50.0f0, kT=1.0f0)],\n)\n\nthermo = ThermodynamicObservable(all; name=:thermo)\n\nsim = Simulation(\n    system;\n    groups=groups,\n    integrator=integrator,\n    observables=[thermo],\n    writers=[\n        TableWriter(\n            \"obs.csv\";\n            every=50,\n            observables=[thermo => [:temperature, :kinetic_energy, :potential_energy]],\n            mode=:replace,\n        ),\n        GSDWriter(\"traj.gsd\"; every=200, group=all, write_start=true, mode=:replace),\n    ],\n    precision=Float32,\n    seed=0xC9A319,\n)\n\nrun!(sim, Stage(:warmup, steps=200; dt=1.0f-4, neighbor_rebuild_interval=1))\nreset_observables!(sim)\nreset_step!(sim, 0)\nrun!(sim, Stage(:production, steps=1_000))","title":"Minimal workflow"},{"category":"section","location":"quickstart/#NVE-production-after-equilibration","page":"Quickstart","text":"Pure NVE on the workflow API is expressed as ConstantVolume(group) with no thermostat. A common pattern is to equilibrate with a thermostat, then swap the integrator and re-prepare before production:\n\nall = Group(:all, AllSelection())\n\nsim = Simulation(\n    system;\n    groups=Groups(all),\n    integrator=Integrator(\n        dt=2.0f-4,\n        forces=[force],\n        methods=[ConstantVolume(all; thermostat=CSVR(kT=1.0f0, tau=0.1f0))],\n    ),\n)\n\nrun!(sim, Stage(:equil, steps=5_000))\n\nsim.integrator = Integrator(\n    dt=2.0f-4,\n    forces=[force],\n    methods=[ConstantVolume(all)],\n)\nprepare!(sim)\nreset_observables!(sim)\nreset_step!(sim, 0)\nrun!(sim, Stage(:production, steps=20_000))\n\nexamples/3D_LJ_NVE.jl is stricter than this pattern: it runs pure NVE from step 0 and sets the initial velocities explicitly on the ParticleSystem.","title":"NVE production after equilibration"},{"category":"section","location":"quickstart/#Groups-apply-methods-and-observables","page":"Quickstart","text":"Groups are currently particle selections. They are designed so the same high-level vocabulary can later grow to bonds, angles, or other topology domains without changing how users think about selection.\n\ncold = Group(:cold, TypeSelection(:C))\nhot = Group(:hot, TypeSelection(:H))\ngroups = Groups(cold, hot, Group(:all, AllSelection()))\n\nMethods and observables attach to those groups:\n\nIntegrator(\n    dt=2.0f-4,\n    scheme=VelocityVerlet(),\n    forces=[force],\n    methods=[\n        Langevin(cold; gamma=50.0f0, kT=5.0f0),\n        Langevin(hot; gamma=50.0f0, kT=100.0f0),\n    ],\n)","title":"Groups apply methods and observables"},{"category":"section","location":"quickstart/#Output-is-scheduled,-not-hand-written","page":"Quickstart","text":"Writers own file management and run on schedules:\n\nEvery(n)\nAtSteps([0, 100, 1_000])\nBetween(first, last; every=...)\n\nThis keeps examples free of manual gsd_open, gsd_close, write_gsd_frame!, manual CSV header logic, and direct collect_step_observables(...) calls.","title":"Output is scheduled, not hand-written"},{"category":"section","location":"quickstart/#Low-level-expert-API","page":"Quickstart","text":"The older expert API is still supported:\n\nbuild_simulation\nstep!\nstep_graph!\nnve, velocityverlet, baoab, baoa, gsm, eulerheun, eulermaruyama, nosehooverchain, csvr\nParticleDynamics.SimulationCore\n\nUse that surface if you need direct control over the GPU-resident SimulationState, debugging of stepping internals, or custom orchestration that the workflow layer intentionally does not expose.","title":"Low-level expert API"},{"category":"section","location":"quickstart/#Current-limitations","page":"Quickstart","text":"CUDA is the main supported backend.\nAngles, dihedrals, impropers, electrostatics, and similar force-field features are future-ready in the vocabulary but not generally implemented.\nForceField is a stable container concept, but compiled support is limited to the existing low-level kernels and wrappers.","title":"Current limitations"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/simulation_state/#Simulation-State","page":"Simulation State","text":"SimulationState is the long-lived, GPU-resident container returned by build_simulation. It owns particle arrays, neighbor data, observables buffers, and optional restart/output metadata for a single simulation.","title":"Simulation State"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/simulation_state/#Core-API","page":"Simulation State","text":"","title":"Core API"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/simulation_state/#What-stays-in-the-state","page":"Simulation State","text":"wrapped positions, velocities, and forces\noptional unwrapped positions\nneighbor-list storage\nbonded and nonbonded parameter storage\nper-particle observables and virial buffers\n\nThe current implementation remains GPU-first: state arrays are CuArray based, and the execution path remains CUDA-specific even though build-time backend selection is now routed through the storage layer.","title":"What stays in the state"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/simulation_state/#Neighbor-initialization-and-capacity","page":"Simulation State","text":"The low-level constructor allocates a dense neighbor container but leaves st.nbh.valid == false until positions have been assigned and a rebuild succeeds. The first simulation force evaluation/step builds the list; callers using standalone force kernels must first call NeighborLists.update_neighbors_inplace!(st.nbh, st.rx, st.ry; box=st.box2) (include st.rz and use st.box3 in 3D). Workflow preparation initializes the coordinates and builds the list automatically.\n\nDense and stencil rows use slot-major ELL storage: zero-based neighbor slot t for one-based particle i is stored at t*N + i. The neighbors_index field remains for compatibility but is not an ELL row offset. Bond adjacency uses a separate CSR representation.\n\ncap is a hard capacity. A build that needs more space throws NeighborLists.NeighborCapacityError and leaves the list invalid, with required_capacity recording the largest required row. Incomplete lists are rejected by force and collision entry points. Rebuild a new container with sufficient capacity, or correct the configuration and rebuild successfully. Rows are never silently truncated. Direct mutation of internal buffers does not maintain these invariants and is unsupported.\n\nst.nbh.valid records successful construction and capacity checking. Simulation force evaluation additionally checks displacement at the actual force positions, including temporary midpoint positions, and rebuilds when needed. Collision sampling checks coverage after each physical position stage and retains the previous sampled contact state across rebuilds. Spatial reordering happens only at step boundaries; midpoint workspaces are never permuted during force calls. neigh_interval controls additional step-boundary maintenance, and no longer allows skipping the mandatory force-time displacement check.","title":"Neighbor initialization and capacity"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/simulation_state/#Cached-forces-and-manual-state-changes","page":"Simulation State","text":"st.force_valid records whether the active force buffer can supply the next initial kick or Brownian drift. Initialization is independent of st.step, so importing a configuration at a nonzero step initializes forces before motion. Changing the visible counter alone does not invalidate a correct cached force.\n\nAfter directly changing coordinates, types, topology, force parameters, or an external provider's internal state, call invalidate_forces!(st). Attaching or detaching a provider, changing freeze controls, copying a workflow system, and applying workflow force setup invalidate the cache automatically. Velocity-only edits do not require force invalidation. For example:\n\ncopyto!(st.rx, new_x)\ncopyto!(st.ry, new_y)\ninvalidate_forces!(st)\n# Also synchronize unwrapped positions if they are enabled.\nsync_unwrapped!(st)\nstep!(st, spec, dt)\n\nInvalidation does not resize neighbor ranges or implement box/model reconfiguration. Such changes still require a correctly configured neighbor container. Raw force kernels do not update the state's cache flag; use the simulation force-evaluation path or invalidate before stepping. Energy and virial freshness are separate from force_valid.\n\nAn exception during a step invalidates the force cache but does not roll back partially advanced positions, velocities, or integrator state. Restore a known configuration before retrying a failed trajectory step.","title":"Cached forces and manual state changes"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/simulation_state/#Minimal-pattern","page":"Simulation State","text":"using ParticleDynamics\n\nst = build_simulation(\n    N=64,\n    box=(40.0f0, 40.0f0),\n    cutoff=Float32(2^(1/6)),\n    skin=0.4f0,\n    cap=Int32(32),\n    epsilon=10.0f0,\n    sigma=1.0f0,\n    gamma=50.0f0,\n    temperature=1.0f0,\n    nonbonded=:wca,\n)","title":"Minimal pattern"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/simulation_state/#Example-scripts","page":"Simulation State","text":"examples/2D_allpairs_quicktest.jl: smallest force-path smoke example, and a likely future CPU-compatible smoke candidate once a CPU backend exists.\nexamples/3D_quicktest.jl: small 3D stepping/output example, also a likely future CPU-compatible smoke candidate.\nexamples/TwoT_2D_LD_VV.jl: larger production-style Langevin setup on the workflow API.","title":"Example scripts"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/simulation_state/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.SimulationState","page":"Simulation State","text":"SimulationState{T}\n\nStructure-of-arrays storage for all GPU-resident buffers required to evolve a non-equilibrium simulation. Users normally do not construct this type manually; build_simulation assembles an allocated instance with concrete CuArray fields and consistent dimensionality inferred from the box argument.\n\nKey fields that user code may read or mutate:\n\nrx, ry[, rz], vx, vy[, vz], fx, fy[, fz]: positions, velocities, and force accumulators in GPU memory. Arrays are sized N and remain allocated during ordinary stepping; optional spatial reordering can replace them. After manual position/force-model edits, call invalidate_forces!(st).\nrx_unwrap, ry_unwrap[, rz_unwrap]: optional unwrapped positions that track continuous motion across periodic boundaries (enabled via unwrapped_positions).\nnbh: neighbor matrix (either dense cell list or sentinel all-pairs) built with the NeighborLists.build_neighbors_* family using the requested cutoff, skin, and capacity.\nmass, dt: nominal build-time timestep plus the scalar fast-path mass used when all particles share one mass. Heterogeneous-mass states additionally populate mass_particle and inv_mass_particle with per-particle data.\nEpot, Ekin, virial, dq, dU plus the corresponding *_accum buffers: energy, virial, and heat observables that can be sampled directly on the GPU.\nvirial_nonbonded, virial_bonded, virial_tensor, virial_tensor_accum: GPU-resident configurational virial tensors. These store the raw pairwise virial W = Σ r_ij ⊗ F_ij using the same minimum-image displacement used in the force kernels. The scalar virial field stores the trace of W for backward compatibility with existing examples. These buffers are refreshed on force evaluations with compute_energy=true.\nvirial_global, virial_global_accum: global virial terms without a per-particle decomposition (the reciprocal-space electrostatic tensor). System totals (virial_tensor, collect_step_observables) include them.\nvdw_modifier: Lennard-Jones energy convention at the cutoff, :none or :potential_shift (see set_vdw_modifier!).\nnonbonded_backend, cluster, cluster_config: short-range traversal selected with set_nonbonded_backend! and its lazily built engine.\ncharge: optional per-particle charges; electrostatics: optional long-range Coulomb solver attached with attach_electrostatics!.\nenergy_valid: true only while the energy and virial buffers belong to the current coordinates, i.e. after a force evaluation with compute_energy=true and before any position change or invalidate_forces!. Force-only evaluations clear it. Workflow observables refresh stale energies before sampling.\ntypeid: per-particle type ids used by Filters and mixed-size LJ kernels.\nfreeze_*: optional buffers used by the freeze/tether helpers in Filters.\ncoll_*: optional buffers that appear when collision counting is enabled via enable_collision_counting!.\n\nAll other fields are internal implementation details (neighbor rebuild state, bond lists, cached LJ parameters, etc.) and should be treated as read-only. Current backend abstraction is storage-level only: SimulationState remains CuArray-backed today, and the execution kernels are still CUDA-specific.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.SimulationState"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/simulation_state/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.build_simulation","page":"Simulation State","text":"build_simulation(; N, box, cutoff=1, skin=0.4, cap=Int32(96),\n                  neigh_interval=20, use_neighborlist=true,\n                  epsilon=1, sigma=1, gamma=1, temperature=1,\n                  noise_corr_time=nothing, dt=0.001,\n                  mass=1, bonds=nothing, bonding=nothing,\n                  nonbonded=:lj, softrep_params=nothing, backend=:cuda,\n                  precision=:f32, unwrapped_positions::Bool=false,\n                  spatial_reorder::Bool=true, reorder_interval::Int=500,\n                  exclude_bonded_pairs::Bool=true, neighbor_cutoff=nothing,\n                  charges=nothing, nonbonded_backend=:ell, prune_buffer=nothing)\n\nConstruct a SimulationState with GPU-resident SoA arrays and a neighbor list configured for the requested potential. All inputs are keyword arguments so that scripts can copy known-good parameter sets directly from the examples/ directory without ambiguity.\n\nKey behaviors:\n\nThe simulation dimensionality (2D vs 3D) is inferred from the length of box. Positions/velocities/forces allocate the corresponding CuArrays.\nFor nonbonded = :wca the neighbor cutoff is forced to the physical WCA value r_c = 2^(1/6) σ even if a larger cutoff was passed, guaranteeing that kernels reuse the validated parameter sets from the packaged examples.\ntemperature accepts either a scalar or length-N vector and is used to initialize velocities. Stochastic integrator parameters are constructed later via explicit specs such as velocityverlet and eulermaruyama.\nmass accepts either a scalar or a length-N vector. Initial velocities are drawn from a Maxwell–Boltzmann distribution with width sqrt(kT / m) for particles with positive mass, then centered to remove center-of-mass drift.\nWhen unwrapped_positions=true, additional rx_unwrap/ry_unwrap/rz_unwrap buffers track continuous positions across periodic boundaries.\nWith spatial_reorder=true (the default) and a dense cell list, NVE runs periodically permute all per-particle arrays into cell-sorted order (at most once per reorder_interval steps, at a neighbor rebuild). This keeps storage order aligned with space, which is essential for force-kernel memory locality at large N. st.tag[k] records the build-time id of the particle in slot k, and GSD/restart output is written in build-time order. Filters.Indices are particle ids and are mapped to current slots through st.tag; typeid-based selections remain valid because typeid is permuted consistently. Pass spatial_reorder=false if external code indexes the state arrays directly by fixed particle number.\nnonbonded = :none builds a state without a short-range vdW term (pure charged or purely bonded systems); cutoff then only sizes the neighbor list.\nneighbor_cutoff widens the neighbor-list radius beyond the vdW cutoff (e.g. to a longer real-space Coulomb cutoff) without changing the vdW potential, which keeps applying its own cutoff.\ncharges (length N, caller's charge unit) allocates st.charge; attach a long-range solver with attach_electrostatics!.\nvdw_modifier = :potential_shift shifts each Lennard-Jones pair energy to zero at its cutoff (forces unchanged; see set_vdw_modifier!).\nnonbonded_backend = :cluster evaluates Lennard-Jones and real-space Coulomb with the fused cluster-pair engine instead of per-particle neighbor rows (3D; see set_nonbonded_backend!, which also documents prune_buffer).\n\nExample (mirrors examples/2D_example.jl, scaled down to N=4096 for testing):\n\nst = build_simulation(N=4096, box=(250f0, 250f0),\n                      cutoff=Float32(2^(1/6)), skin=Float32(2^(1/6))/2,\n                      cap=Int32(250), neigh_interval=50,\n                      epsilon=1f4, sigma=1f0,\n                      gamma=615f0, temperature=10f0,\n                      dt=1f-5, nonbonded=:wca, precision=:f32)\ncopyto!(st.rx, Float32[3 * mod(i - 1, 64) - 95 for i in 1:4096])\ncopyto!(st.ry, Float32[3 * ((i - 1) ÷ 64) - 95 for i in 1:4096])\nvv = velocityverlet(st; gamma=615f0, temperature=10f0, dt=1f-5)\nstep!(st, vv, 1f-5; compute_energy=false)\n\nReturns an allocated SimulationState. Assign particle coordinates before the first step; the initial dense neighbor build is deferred until the first force evaluation or explicit neighbor update. Stochastic controls such as noise_corr_time now belong to the explicit integrator spec rather than the core simulation state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.build_simulation"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/simulation_state/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.sync_unwrapped!","page":"Simulation State","text":"sync_unwrapped!(st)\n\nCopy the wrapped positions into the unwrapped buffers. Call this after manually overwriting st.rx/st.ry/st.rz when unwrapped_positions=true.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.sync_unwrapped!"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/simulation_state/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.step!","page":"Simulation State","text":"step!(st, spec, dt; compute_energy=true)\n\nCanonical explicit stepping API. Every step runs on the device engine (ParticleDynamics.DeviceEngine adds the methods for each integrator); there is no staged fallback.\n\n\n\n\n\nstep!(st, vv, dt; compute_energy=true)\n\nCompatibility wrapper for explicit VVParams stepping.\n\n\n\n\n\nstep!(st, bao, dt; compute_energy=true)\n\nCompatibility wrapper for explicit BAOABParams stepping.\n\n\n\n\n\nstep!(st, bp, dt; compute_energy=true)\n\nCompatibility wrapper for explicit Brownian midpoint stepping.\n\n\n\n\n\nstep!(st, em, dt; compute_energy=true)\n\nCompatibility wrapper for explicit Euler-Maruyama stepping.\n\n\n\n\n\nstep!(st, nhc, dt; compute_energy=true)\n\nCompatibility wrapper for explicit Nose-Hoover Chain parameter stepping.\n\n\n\n\n\nstep!(st, csvr_params, dt; compute_energy=true)\n\nCompatibility wrapper for explicit CSVR parameter stepping.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.step!"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/simulation_state/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.step_graph!","page":"Simulation State","text":"step_graph!(st, spec, dt; compute_energy=true)\n\nSame as step!: every step is a captured CUDA-graph replay on the device engine.\n\n\n\n\n\nstep_graph!(st, vv, dt; compute_energy=true)\n\nCompatibility wrapper for explicit VVParams graph stepping.\n\n\n\n\n\nstep_graph!(st, bao, dt; compute_energy=true)\n\nCompatibility wrapper for explicit BAOABParams graph stepping.\n\n\n\n\n\nstep_graph!(st, bp, dt; compute_energy=true)\n\nCompatibility wrapper for explicit Brownian midpoint graph stepping.\n\n\n\n\n\nstep_graph!(st, em, dt; compute_energy=true)\n\nCompatibility wrapper for explicit Euler-Maruyama graph stepping.\n\n\n\n\n\nstep_graph!(st, nhc, dt; compute_energy=true)\n\nCompatibility wrapper for explicit Nose-Hoover chain graph stepping.\n\n\n\n\n\nstep_graph!(st, csvr_params, dt; compute_energy=true)\n\nCompatibility wrapper for explicit CSVR graph stepping.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.step_graph!"},{"category":"section","location":"legacy/collision_rate/#Collision-Rate-Notes-(Legacy)","page":"Collision Rate Notes","text":"The historical collision-rate derivation note remains available at:\n\ndocs/CollisionRate.md\n\nThis page is kept as a pointer while the new manual collision documentation is being consolidated.","title":"Collision Rate Notes (Legacy)"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/restarts/#Restarts","page":"Restarts","text":"Restart support currently centers on reading GSD frames back into a structured host-side container that can be used to rebuild a simulation.","title":"Restarts"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/restarts/#Public-restart-API","page":"Restarts","text":"","title":"Public restart API"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/restarts/#What-read_gsd_frame!-returns","page":"Restarts","text":"read_gsd_frame! returns a GSDFrameData object that:\n\npreserves legacy tuple-style destructuring for older scripts\nexposes particle arrays, topology, and metadata through named fields\nkeeps restart parsing outside the hot CUDA stepping path\n\nThe metadata container types themselves are documented on the I/O page:\n\nParticleDynamics.Writers.GSDFrameData\nParticleDynamics.Writers.GSDTopology","title":"What read_gsd_frame! returns"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/restarts/#Minimal-pattern","page":"Restarts","text":"frame = read_gsd_frame!(\"traj.gsd\")\n@show frame.step frame.N frame.box","title":"Minimal pattern"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/restarts/#Related-scripts","page":"Restarts","text":"examples/3D_quicktest.jl for write-side GSD usage\nexamples/restart_from_gsd.jl for the high-level restart workflow wrapper\nrestart-oriented user workflows should treat current examples as GPU-first; later CPU-compatible restart smoke tests can be added after a CPU backend exists","title":"Related scripts"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/restarts/#ParticleDynamics.Writers.read_gsd_frame!","page":"Restarts","text":"Read a valid frame from a GSD file and return a GSDFrameData.\n\nThe returned object can still be destructured into the legacy tuple step, rx, ry, rz, vx, vy, vz, typeid, types, box, force for backwards compatibility, while also exposing rich metadata (frame.topology, frame.particle_properties, frame.configuration, …) that can be fed directly into ParticleDynamics initialisation routines.\n\nArguments:\n\nfile_path: path to the GSD file.\nstep: optional timestep or frame index to target. When nothing, the most recent complete frame is returned. When provided, the routine first searches for a frame whose configuration/step matches and, if none exist, treats the value as a 1-based frame index.\n\nThrows:\n\nArgumentError if mandatory chunks are missing or no readable frame exists.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Writers.read_gsd_frame!"},{"category":"section","location":"#ParticleDynamics.jl","page":"Home","text":"ParticleDynamics.jl is a GPU-first Julia package for non-equilibrium particle simulations on CUDA.jl. The recommended public surface is the workflow API: ParticleSystem, Group, Force, Integrator, Observable, Writer, Stage, Simulation, and run!.\n\n<div style=\"padding:0.6rem 0.8rem; border-left:4px solid #d9534f; background:#fff6f6;\">\n<strong>GPU-only package:</strong> a functional CUDA environment is required for simulations and test-validated behavior.\n</div>","title":"ParticleDynamics.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"The current release is 0.6.10, registered in the Julia General registry:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.add(\"ParticleDynamics\")\n\nSee the integrator and force manuals for the timestep and physical-domain requirements.","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Scope","page":"Home","text":"2D/3D particle dynamics with periodic boundaries.\nDeterministic MD: NVE, thermostatted NVT, and NPT with stochastic cell rescaling.\nLangevin integrators: velocity-Verlet (default), BAOAB, BAOA, GSM.\nBrownian integrators: midpoint (Euler-Heun style) and Euler-Maruyama.\nNonbonded interactions (LJ/WCA/soft-repulsive) with exact or buffered neighbor lists, GPU long-range electrostatics (PME, PPPM, Ewald summation with prolates), and machine-learned potentials (MACE, Orb).\nBonded interactions: bonds (harmonic/FENE), angles, dihedrals and impropers; rigid three-site water.\nEnergy minimization (FIRE, steepest descent), filters/freeze controls, collision counting, and XYZ/CSV/GSD outputs.","title":"Scope"},{"category":"section","location":"#Workflow-model","page":"Home","text":"ParticleSystem stores positions, types, masses, box, velocities, and topology.\nGroup selects particles today and is designed to grow into other topology domains later.\nForce objects describe physical interactions.\nIntegrator owns dt, forces, methods, and thermostats.\nObservable objects compute sampled quantities.\nWriter objects own scheduled output.\nStage describes a named block of running.\nSimulation assembles the workflow, and run! owns the timestep loop.","title":"Workflow model"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-Links","page":"Home","text":"Workflow start: Quickstart\nExpert API: Low-Level API\nState layout: Simulation State\nIntegrators: Integrators\nInteractions: Forces\nSelection and controls: Groups and Filters\nThermostat interfaces: Thermostats\nDiagnostics: Observables\nOutput formats: I/O\nRestart reading: Restarts\nLegacy notes: Collision Rate Notes\nExample inventory: examples/README.md","title":"Quick Links"},{"category":"section","location":"#Public-API-map","page":"Home","text":"The supported top-level API is intentionally split into a workflow layer and a low-level expert layer.\n\nAPI group Exported symbols Primary page\nWorkflow Simulation, ParticleSystem, Topology, Group, Groups, Force objects, Integrator, Observable objects, Writer objects, Stage, prepare!, run!, reset_step!, reset_observables!, state quickstart.md\nLow-level expert API SimulationState, build_simulation, step!, step_graph!, sync_unwrapped!, collect_step_observables, reset_bath_exchange_accumulators!, nve, velocityverlet, baoab, baoa, gsm, eulerheun, eulermaruyama, nosehooverchain, csvr manual/getting_started.md, manual/simulation_state.md, manual/integrators.md\nParameters and physical helpers LJParams, SoftRepulsiveParams, HarmonicBondParams, FENEParams, BondPotential, HarmonicBond, FENEBond, harmonic_bond, fene_bond, StokesFrictionCoefficient, SphereMass, InertialTime, DiffusiveTime manual/forces.md\nInitialization generators box_from_phi_2d, box_from_phi_3d, hex_random_2d, hex_circle_2d, hex_circle_plus_random_2d, hex_sites_in_box_2d, hex_circle_in_box_2d, hex_slab_coexistence_2d, fcc_sites_in_box_3d, fcc_random_3d, fcc_slab_coexistence_3d manual/getting_started.md\nWriters and I/O write_xyz!, write_observables_csv!, gsd_open, gsd_close, write_gsd_frame!, read_gsd_frame!, InMemoryLogger, CSVWriter, XYZWriter manual/io.md\nBond lists BondList, build_bondlist manual/forces.md\nCollision counting enable_collision_counting!, disable_collision_counting!, collisions_reset_counts!, collisions_read_counts!, set_collision_pair_cutoffs! manual/observables.md\nSelection helpers and modules ParticleSelection, ParticleGroup, All, TypeIDs, Indices, materialize, apply_scalar!, apply_values!, gather, sum_values, plus the exported Filters/BondedForces modules for qualified access manual/groups_filters.md, manual/forces.md\n\nAdvanced helpers that are intentionally not part of the default import surface live under ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore, for example ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.zero_forces!, ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.accumulate_energies!, ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.thermostatted_dof, and ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.thermostatted_particle_mask.","title":"Public API map"},{"category":"section","location":"#Behavior-Baseline-(from-tests)","page":"Home","text":"Current tests validate:\n\nDeterministic force/kernel properties (analytic 2-particle checks, Newton symmetry, PBC invariance, backend parity).\nStochastic behavior at moment level (Brownian MSD slope, Langevin equipartition, OU trend checks, weak-step trend in deterministic limit).\nRegression/IR fixes including gamma > 0 enforced error behavior on stochastic paths where required.","title":"Behavior Baseline (from tests)"},{"category":"section","location":"#Current-limitations","page":"Home","text":"CUDA is the primary supported backend.\nAngles, dihedrals, impropers, electrostatics, and broader force-field concepts are future-facing vocabulary unless already implemented in the existing low-level engine.\nForceField is stable as a container concept, but compiled support remains limited to the force families backed by current kernels and wrappers.","title":"Current limitations"},{"category":"section","location":"#Build-docs-locally","page":"Home","text":"julia --project=docs -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate(); include(\"docs/make.jl\")'","title":"Build docs locally"}]
}
